1
00:00:39,748 --> 00:00:43,126
There's a story told in the town of
Big Lake, Texas.

2
00:00:44,002 --> 00:00:46,462
A story of the town's beginnings.

3
00:00:46,964 --> 00:00:49,882
lt's the story
of the Santa Rita number one,

4
00:00:50,175 --> 00:00:53,219
the birthplace of west Texas oil.

5
00:00:53,679 --> 00:00:58,015
Now, as with most Texas legends,
there was a man who thought

6
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,476
oil was right beneath his feet.

7
00:01:01,478 --> 00:01:03,980
And as with most such stories,

8
00:01:04,398 --> 00:01:06,023
no one else believed him.

9
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:12,655
Except, in this case, for two nuns,

10
00:01:13,157 --> 00:01:16,617
who in 1923 met the man

11
00:01:16,702 --> 00:01:19,912
and believed so much
in him and his dream

12
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:23,541
that they invested money
no one knew they had.

13
00:01:32,092 --> 00:01:35,094
Now, when the nuns told
their parish priest

14
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:38,598
about the man's dream
and their investment in it,

15
00:01:39,558 --> 00:01:43,519
now, he counselled them
to try and get their money back.

16
00:01:43,604 --> 00:01:47,064
Sheepishly, they admitted that
it was too late.

17
00:01:47,149 --> 00:01:49,567
That the money was already spent.

18
00:01:51,445 --> 00:01:53,446
Well, the priest, he sighed,

19
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:57,867
shook his head and offered
just one small bit of advice.

20
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:03,247
Bless the site with rose petals

21
00:02:03,332 --> 00:02:05,917
and invoke the help of Saint Rita,

22
00:02:06,627 --> 00:02:10,421
patron saint of impossible dreams.

23
00:02:20,557 --> 00:02:25,311
And while the workers waited
for the oil that would eventually come,

24
00:02:25,395 --> 00:02:27,355
they played baseball.

25
00:02:28,315 --> 00:02:29,649
He's outta here.

26
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:33,069
Played baseball so well,
that some were able to give up

27
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:37,365
the dirt and the despair,
and went on to play major league ball,

28
00:02:37,449 --> 00:02:40,743
in the glory days of Ruth and Gehrig.

29
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:24,245
l've heard and told that story
on many occasions,

30
00:03:24,329 --> 00:03:29,292
but that was long before Saint Rita
decided to bless our little town

31
00:03:29,376 --> 00:03:31,294
just one more time.

32
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:34,797
Yeah!

33
00:03:43,098 --> 00:03:45,516
First game in the big leagues,

34
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:48,227
and Jimmy Morris has
a no-hitter going.

35
00:03:52,441 --> 00:03:55,610
Jimmy, don't throw it so hard.
My hands are cold.

36
00:04:02,284 --> 00:04:03,951
-What are you doing?
-Waiting till spring,

37
00:04:04,036 --> 00:04:05,912
that's what I'm doing.

38
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:08,998
Jimmy? Your father
and I need to talk to you.

39
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:15,046
I hear Virginia's real nice.

40
00:04:16,673 --> 00:04:20,301
At least that's what the Navy folks
told your father.

41
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:24,388
-Isn't that right, Jim?
-We'll just be moving again.

42
00:04:24,473 --> 00:04:28,100
It's my job to decide when we move.
It's yourjob to make the best of it.

43
00:04:28,185 --> 00:04:29,852
Jim? Please.

44
00:04:32,522 --> 00:04:36,525
Who knows? Maybe it won't
snow so much in Virginia.

45
00:04:36,652 --> 00:04:38,235
Jimmy Morris

46
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:41,197
with another
masterful pitching performance,

47
00:04:42,157 --> 00:04:46,410
well on his way
to another World Series title.

48
00:05:02,886 --> 00:05:06,764
Jimmy! Your father
and I need to talk to you.

49
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:42,425
Strike three!

50
00:05:46,930 --> 00:05:48,055
Hey.

51
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:49,598
Jimmy.

52
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:58,983
-Hey, partner. How ya doing?
-Hey.

53
00:06:11,371 --> 00:06:13,497
-Hey, Dad.
-Jimmy.

54
00:06:21,089 --> 00:06:24,300
I had a good game. Thirteen strikeouts.

55
00:06:24,885 --> 00:06:26,385
Did you win?

56
00:06:35,937 --> 00:06:39,732
Just found out there's a chance
we may be restationed again.

57
00:06:41,318 --> 00:06:44,070
-How much of a chance?
-It's in West Texas.

58
00:06:44,154 --> 00:06:46,739
The Navy wants me to be
a recruiter there.

59
00:06:46,823 --> 00:06:48,365
Texas?

60
00:06:48,450 --> 00:06:50,201
-When?
-Three weeks.

61
00:06:52,329 --> 00:06:54,955
But, Dad, we still got
half our season to play.

62
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:56,707
I'm aware of that.

63
00:06:56,792 --> 00:06:59,335
Well, maybe I could stay
with the Johnsons.

64
00:06:59,419 --> 00:07:01,170
We move as a family.

65
00:07:09,179 --> 00:07:11,430
I don't have a choice in this.

66
00:07:16,895 --> 00:07:19,396
What kind of baseball do they have?

67
00:07:19,981 --> 00:07:21,482
They don't.

68
00:07:24,111 --> 00:07:27,196
I hear they might be starting
some summer ball.

69
00:07:28,615 --> 00:07:29,865
Yes, sir.

70
00:07:56,017 --> 00:07:57,309
What are those things?

71
00:07:57,394 --> 00:08:00,146
-What?
-Those things moving up and down.

72
00:08:00,772 --> 00:08:02,189
Oil rigs.

73
00:08:02,274 --> 00:08:06,235
When those things are moving
up and down, means times are good.

74
00:08:07,070 --> 00:08:09,113
Looks like times are good.

75
00:08:52,616 --> 00:08:54,909
Shoot.

76
00:08:54,993 --> 00:08:57,244
We must have left some of them
back in Florida.

77
00:08:57,329 --> 00:08:59,997
-Left what?
-I packed a couple of boxes

78
00:09:00,081 --> 00:09:02,875
with socks and things.
I could've sworn...

79
00:09:02,959 --> 00:09:06,712
Jimmy, did you find that one box?
Oh, no.

80
00:09:07,797 --> 00:09:11,133
-It's okay, Mom.
-Don't worry, honey, we'll find it.

81
00:09:13,303 --> 00:09:16,013
I've never known a boy to get so upset
about losing his socks.

82
00:09:16,097 --> 00:09:17,890
His glove was in there.

83
00:09:17,974 --> 00:09:20,643
-His what?
-His glove was in there.

84
00:09:23,313 --> 00:09:25,606
Quit moping and grab a box.

85
00:09:26,358 --> 00:09:28,192
Did you hear me?

86
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:33,239
There are more important things
in life than baseball.

87
00:09:33,323 --> 00:09:36,158
The sooner you figure that out,
the better.

88
00:10:07,065 --> 00:10:10,401
This Lee Ivaco-cocus guy,

89
00:10:10,485 --> 00:10:13,737
I guarantee you, within two years,
three years tops,

90
00:10:13,822 --> 00:10:16,949
he's gonna run that company
right out of business.

91
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:19,201
Ain't that right, young man?

92
00:10:22,163 --> 00:10:23,747
Eileen?

93
00:10:24,082 --> 00:10:25,666
Eileen!

94
00:10:27,627 --> 00:10:29,878
I guess she gone to lunch.

95
00:10:34,342 --> 00:10:36,552
Help you find something, son?

96
00:10:36,803 --> 00:10:39,013
-Socks.
-Socks we have.

97
00:10:39,097 --> 00:10:40,723
In contrast to common sense,

98
00:10:40,807 --> 00:10:43,726
which we seem to be running
a little short on today.

99
00:10:43,810 --> 00:10:46,812
I'll be right down.
Don't look at my dominoes.

100
00:10:49,065 --> 00:10:50,482
All right, sir, we got white socks

101
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:52,860
with red stripes, black stripes,
blue stripes.

102
00:10:52,944 --> 00:10:56,363
Over there you got all your solids
in black, blue, brown and grey.

103
00:10:56,448 --> 00:10:58,240
Just take your pick.

104
00:10:58,450 --> 00:11:01,035
That's a real nice pair, right there.

105
00:11:01,786 --> 00:11:03,454
You visiting?

106
00:11:03,538 --> 00:11:07,333
-We just moved here.
-Oh, well, then I'm Henry Sampson.

107
00:11:07,417 --> 00:11:09,585
Nice to meet you. Jimmy Morris.

108
00:11:09,669 --> 00:11:12,254
Welcome to Big Lake,
Mr Jimmy Morris.

109
00:11:33,234 --> 00:11:34,902
You know, son, I can probably
tell you where it is

110
00:11:34,986 --> 00:11:37,488
a whole lot quicker than you can find it.

111
00:11:42,911 --> 00:11:46,205
-Got any baseball stuff?
-Baseball stuff.

112
00:11:47,290 --> 00:11:50,417
We don't get much call
for baseball stuff around these parts.

113
00:11:50,502 --> 00:11:54,129
Now if it was football,
that'd be a whole different story.

114
00:11:58,385 --> 00:12:00,302
You know something, though?
I got a catalogue over here

115
00:12:00,387 --> 00:12:04,181
that just might help us out some.
Let's take a look at that.

116
00:12:09,896 --> 00:12:12,940
Yeah, I think we might find something
in here.

117
00:12:13,566 --> 00:12:16,068
You know, if you're interested
in baseball,

118
00:12:16,152 --> 00:12:18,570
there's a story that people here
in this town tell

119
00:12:18,655 --> 00:12:20,614
that you probably ought to hear.

120
00:12:20,698 --> 00:12:24,034
I thought you said nobody here
cared about baseball.

121
00:12:24,327 --> 00:12:26,328
They care about this.

122
00:12:27,497 --> 00:12:30,124
It all started with a couple of nuns

123
00:12:31,334 --> 00:12:34,169
and piece of ground
not too far from here.

124
00:15:06,990 --> 00:15:08,949
-Morning, Esther.
-Morning, Jimmy.

125
00:15:09,033 --> 00:15:10,576
Any more rattlesnakes
on the playground?

126
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:13,412
Yeah. Two this morning.
I swear those things show up

127
00:15:13,496 --> 00:15:15,122
every time the oil business goes bad.

128
00:15:15,206 --> 00:15:17,916
-They gonna bring the kids in?
-They're thinking about it.

129
00:15:18,001 --> 00:15:21,086
Don't these families
have enough to worry about?

130
00:15:21,462 --> 00:15:23,171
-Coach.
-Counsellor.

131
00:15:28,052 --> 00:15:30,762
-Don't report that, Esther.
-I never do.

132
00:15:33,349 --> 00:15:37,019
Sum means that we're adding.
We have at least two items.

133
00:15:38,605 --> 00:15:41,440
Don't make this hard,
but what are we adding here?

134
00:15:41,524 --> 00:15:43,609
-Protons and...
-Neutrons?

135
00:15:43,693 --> 00:15:46,403
Neutrons. Neutrons.

136
00:15:46,821 --> 00:15:50,616
So, David, these protons
and these neutrons,

137
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:52,951
they're inside of what?

138
00:15:55,121 --> 00:15:57,623
-The nucleus?
-Well, don't say it like a question, son.

139
00:15:57,707 --> 00:16:00,834
If you think you know it,
just say it like you know it.

140
00:16:00,918 --> 00:16:03,545
-The nucleus.
-That's right. The nucleus.

141
00:16:04,464 --> 00:16:06,840
So, congratulations.
Go home tonight and tell your parents

142
00:16:06,924 --> 00:16:09,468
that you figured out
the mass number of an atom.

143
00:16:11,888 --> 00:16:14,348
Frank, ain't it a little late
to be playing this song?

144
00:16:14,432 --> 00:16:16,391
-It's 8:30.
-It's March.

145
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:19,478
Well, that song's too good
to play for only one month.

146
00:16:19,562 --> 00:16:21,063
Hunter, you gonna want
cheese on that?

147
00:16:21,147 --> 00:16:22,773
-Yes, sir.
-Besides,

148
00:16:22,857 --> 00:16:25,067
-he smoked three packs a day.
-Who?

149
00:16:25,151 --> 00:16:27,653
Nat King Cole.
Thought it'd make his voice deeper.

150
00:16:27,737 --> 00:16:30,572
And Frank Sinatra was
the poster child for vocal care?

151
00:16:30,657 --> 00:16:33,659
Who died in his 40s?
You take care of yourself,

152
00:16:33,743 --> 00:16:36,453
-things work out.
-Is that right, is it?

153
00:16:36,537 --> 00:16:40,332
Well, what about Jimmy then? I mean,
he took real good care of himself.

154
00:16:40,416 --> 00:16:42,584
And, how many surgeries
was it, Jimmy? Four?

155
00:16:42,669 --> 00:16:46,004
They put this thing
from his ankle up to his shoulder.

156
00:16:47,090 --> 00:16:49,716
We're talking about singing,
not pitching.

157
00:16:49,801 --> 00:16:53,303
And, Hunter? Do us all a favour
and don't tell us that bit

158
00:16:53,388 --> 00:16:55,847
-about the ankle any more.
-Mel Tormé in June.

159
00:16:55,932 --> 00:16:57,516
What in the world
are you talking about now?

160
00:16:57,600 --> 00:17:01,103
Mel Tormé wrote The Christmas Song
on a beach somewhere.

161
00:17:01,187 --> 00:17:05,023
There wasn't no Jack Frost
nipping at anybody's nose that day.

162
00:17:08,945 --> 00:17:10,320
-Daddy?
-Yeah?

163
00:17:10,405 --> 00:17:12,614
Your arm ever hurt any more?

164
00:17:13,324 --> 00:17:14,700
Only when I have to
drag you out of bed

165
00:17:14,784 --> 00:17:16,827
in the morning for school.

166
00:17:17,787 --> 00:17:20,330
No, I'm just kidding you, bud.
It hasn't hurt in a long time.

167
00:17:20,415 --> 00:17:21,957
How long?

168
00:17:24,293 --> 00:17:27,754
-Why do you want to know?
-Just wondering.

169
00:17:30,299 --> 00:17:32,134
Let's see.

170
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:37,723
It never hurt when I was in high school
'cause we didn't have a baseball team.

171
00:17:37,807 --> 00:17:40,559
It never hurt when I was in
junior college 'cause

172
00:17:40,643 --> 00:17:42,936
well, that's why I got drafted.

173
00:17:43,229 --> 00:17:47,065
And then, I don't know.
Just started hurting.

174
00:17:50,027 --> 00:17:52,446
Is that why you didn't make it?

175
00:17:56,868 --> 00:17:58,744
It's never one thing.

176
00:18:56,052 --> 00:18:58,136
I'll get her.

177
00:18:58,221 --> 00:19:00,639
Is that a real "I'll get her,"

178
00:19:00,723 --> 00:19:03,934
or you just waiting for me to say,
"Go back to sleep?"

179
00:19:04,018 --> 00:19:05,894
I haven't decided yet.

180
00:19:07,104 --> 00:19:08,897
Go back to sleep.

181
00:19:18,324 --> 00:19:20,534
What's all that crying about?

182
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:24,621
I think it's my turn.

183
00:19:24,705 --> 00:19:27,249
No. It's your turn to sleep.

184
00:19:29,043 --> 00:19:30,335
Okay.

185
00:19:31,754 --> 00:19:34,422
-You sure?
-Yeah.

186
00:19:36,551 --> 00:19:38,677
Here we go. Come on, baby.

187
00:19:38,761 --> 00:19:40,262
There we are.

188
00:19:40,805 --> 00:19:43,348
-Good night.
-Good night.

189
00:19:46,978 --> 00:20:03,910
There we go.

190
00:20:17,884 --> 00:20:20,719
Hey, Rudy,
when was the last date you had?

191
00:20:21,137 --> 00:20:22,554
What, like a year ago?

192
00:20:22,638 --> 00:20:24,598
-It's enough for me, man.
-Come on, man.

193
00:20:24,682 --> 00:20:27,726
Who you kidding? You, too, Rudy.
Don't be laughing.

194
00:20:27,810 --> 00:20:29,227
-All set, Coach.
-You're not the only one.

195
00:20:29,312 --> 00:20:30,896
Give me a ball.

196
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:37,569
-Hey, Coach, I forgot my glove.
-Check the bag.

197
00:20:37,653 --> 00:20:39,613
-Ten players, nine gloves.
-Yeah.

198
00:20:39,697 --> 00:20:41,990
-How do they look?
-Couple of holes.

199
00:20:42,074 --> 00:20:44,367
-All right, get one.
-Thanks, man.

200
00:20:46,871 --> 00:20:50,332
Hey, Coach. They putting down
any grass seed in the field this year?

201
00:20:50,416 --> 00:20:52,959
Man, it's a goat track out here.

202
00:20:53,044 --> 00:20:56,880
-Just waiting for the seed to take.
-What seed?

203
00:20:58,007 --> 00:21:00,508
-Ain't no seed out here, man.
-Wack!

204
00:21:02,386 --> 00:21:04,262
How come we get dirt
and the football field looks

205
00:21:04,347 --> 00:21:07,432
-like Tiger Woods' backyard?
-They don't even play for six months.

206
00:21:07,516 --> 00:21:10,226
Just let me worry about
the field, all right?

207
00:21:10,311 --> 00:21:13,730
-You plan on worrying anytime soon?
-All right, Wack.

208
00:21:13,814 --> 00:21:16,274
You can get your running done
early today.

209
00:21:16,359 --> 00:21:20,445
-All right, I'll get my running in early.
-Looking good.

210
00:21:21,989 --> 00:21:23,907
Okay, bring it home!

211
00:21:30,957 --> 00:21:33,166
Hey, Coach? You wanna throw?

212
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:37,921
You know, I've seen you out there
at the old Legion Field at night.

213
00:21:38,005 --> 00:21:39,798
You know, sometimes
it helps when you throw

214
00:21:39,882 --> 00:21:44,886
if you got somebody catching for you.
Ball, glove. That kind of thing.

215
00:21:47,807 --> 00:21:50,141
-I'll throw a couple.
-Really?

216
00:21:52,311 --> 00:21:53,687
All right.

217
00:21:59,527 --> 00:22:02,320
How come you throw out there?
All by yourself?

218
00:22:02,405 --> 00:22:03,905
Helps me relax.

219
00:22:03,990 --> 00:22:05,699
I've been doing it

220
00:22:06,450 --> 00:22:08,284
pretty long time.

221
00:22:13,833 --> 00:22:16,042
Coach, back when you were
really throwing,

222
00:22:16,127 --> 00:22:20,839
-how fast were you bringing it?
-I don't know. 85, 86.

223
00:22:20,923 --> 00:22:22,007
Well, that's not bad.

224
00:22:22,091 --> 00:22:26,094
Well, it is when the other guy's
throwing 90.

225
00:22:27,471 --> 00:22:30,223
Coach, come on.
Let's see you bring one.

226
00:22:30,307 --> 00:22:32,142
Can't.

227
00:22:32,226 --> 00:22:33,852
Promised too many doctors.

228
00:22:33,936 --> 00:22:36,771
Come on, Coach,
one is not gonna kill you.

229
00:22:38,941 --> 00:22:43,028
-Now, come on. Feed me.
-Yeah, Dad, bring the heat.

230
00:22:47,491 --> 00:22:49,075
Feed me, Coach.

231
00:22:58,127 --> 00:22:59,794
-Wow!
-Coach.

232
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:05,633
-Where'd that come from?
-Forgot how good that sounded.

233
00:23:08,846 --> 00:23:10,388
Yes.

234
00:23:46,342 --> 00:23:47,842
Unbelievable.

235
00:23:50,554 --> 00:23:53,598
Coach, how fast do you think
those were coming?

236
00:23:53,766 --> 00:23:55,975
Not as fast as you think.

237
00:23:56,352 --> 00:23:58,269
Hey, do me a favour, all right?

238
00:23:58,354 --> 00:24:00,146
Let's just keep this
between you and me.

239
00:24:00,231 --> 00:24:03,441
-Okay.
-You could barely see that last one.

240
00:24:03,526 --> 00:24:06,111
And that goes for you, too, all right?
No telling Mom.

241
00:24:06,195 --> 00:24:07,529
Why?

242
00:24:09,198 --> 00:24:10,865
Because I said so.

243
00:24:22,336 --> 00:24:24,087
Good night, Ray.

244
00:24:24,171 --> 00:24:26,714
I know why your grass isn't growing.

245
00:24:38,727 --> 00:24:41,688
Usually get three or four out here
every night.

246
00:24:41,772 --> 00:24:44,482
So I put the seed down, they pick it up.

247
00:24:45,317 --> 00:24:48,236
Once it comes in,
they don't like it as much.

248
00:24:48,779 --> 00:24:51,447
That's why the football field
looks so good.

249
00:24:51,532 --> 00:24:53,241
Yeah?

250
00:24:53,325 --> 00:24:55,410
Maybe those football guys
are paying off these deer

251
00:24:55,494 --> 00:24:58,246
with all the big budget money they got.

252
00:25:02,418 --> 00:25:05,378
Hey! Go on! Get out of here!

253
00:25:12,261 --> 00:25:15,138
I thought deer
were supposed to be skittish.

254
00:25:20,269 --> 00:25:22,353
You need me to top that off?

255
00:25:23,606 --> 00:25:26,024
Nothing like wine bought
with a Chevron credit card.

256
00:25:26,108 --> 00:25:28,610
Well, I know what a demanding
consumer you can be.

257
00:25:29,278 --> 00:25:31,279
-Yeah?
-Yeah.

258
00:25:42,166 --> 00:25:43,708
I threw today.

259
00:25:45,502 --> 00:25:47,545
To Joel. After practise.

260
00:25:48,964 --> 00:25:50,798
-You threw?
-I threw.

261
00:25:52,092 --> 00:25:53,343
How hard?

262
00:25:54,803 --> 00:25:56,262
Pretty hard.

263
00:26:05,773 --> 00:26:08,816
Don't worry. If it starts to hurt, I'll stop.

264
00:26:08,901 --> 00:26:10,652
All right? Promise.

265
00:26:13,072 --> 00:26:15,531
I gotta check on the kids.

266
00:26:21,288 --> 00:26:23,915
Wack, got the muffler changed, buddy.

267
00:26:23,999 --> 00:26:27,543
The Jeep. Can't hear me coming
three miles away, can you there?

268
00:26:27,628 --> 00:26:31,005
-Ray, check out the screwball!
-Ladies loving the Jeep.

269
00:26:31,632 --> 00:26:33,925
Come on, man. Give me the rock.

270
00:26:37,346 --> 00:26:39,889
All right, bring it in for some BP.

271
00:26:39,974 --> 00:26:41,891
We can't. Rudy's not here.

272
00:26:41,976 --> 00:26:43,559
-Well, where is he?
-Locker room.

273
00:26:43,644 --> 00:26:46,437
Something to do
with only having one shoe.

274
00:26:49,900 --> 00:26:53,611
-Somebody grab a bat.
-You're throwing batting practise?

275
00:26:53,696 --> 00:26:55,154
Yeah.

276
00:26:55,239 --> 00:26:57,282
-I'm up first, man.
-I am next.

277
00:26:57,366 --> 00:26:59,534
-All right.
-I hit the Lotto!

278
00:27:01,495 --> 00:27:04,205
Come on, Wack! Send it, baby!
Send it!

279
00:27:04,290 --> 00:27:06,332
What do you say, Wack?

280
00:27:12,589 --> 00:27:14,048
Yeah.

281
00:27:14,133 --> 00:27:16,926
Looks like that one's gonna land
in another time zone, Coach!

282
00:27:17,011 --> 00:27:19,762
You might wanna reset your watch
before this next one.

283
00:27:19,847 --> 00:27:21,389
You see that?

284
00:27:21,974 --> 00:27:23,516
Yeah, it's...

285
00:27:26,228 --> 00:27:27,562
Come on.

286
00:27:31,608 --> 00:27:33,026
Yeah.

287
00:27:45,122 --> 00:27:46,664
What was that?

288
00:27:47,416 --> 00:27:48,499
Man!

289
00:27:49,043 --> 00:27:50,918
Wait till he warms up.

290
00:27:54,590 --> 00:27:56,591
-Let me see another one of those.
-Nope.

291
00:27:56,675 --> 00:27:59,635
It's batting practise,
not pitching practise.

292
00:28:00,888 --> 00:28:02,221
All right.

293
00:28:02,931 --> 00:28:05,224
Oh, my God!

294
00:28:05,934 --> 00:28:08,102
I'm already down two bits.

295
00:28:09,146 --> 00:28:12,940
How many of them job applications
you plan on sending out, Jimmy?

296
00:28:13,025 --> 00:28:15,526
Many as it takes, I guess.

297
00:28:15,611 --> 00:28:18,363
So, the little boy coming into my store
to buy socks

298
00:28:18,447 --> 00:28:22,742
after a dozen moves is planning on
making a move of his own now?

299
00:28:22,826 --> 00:28:26,329
-Well, this is different.
-It always is.

300
00:28:26,413 --> 00:28:29,374
I hear you diagnosed that problem
with that baseball field of yours.

301
00:28:29,458 --> 00:28:33,002
-You mean his potential field.
-Well, diagnosed part's right.

302
00:28:33,087 --> 00:28:34,712
It's the curing part
I'm having trouble with.

303
00:28:34,797 --> 00:28:36,381
-I'll take five.
-You know, I had the same trouble

304
00:28:36,465 --> 00:28:39,509
-out at my place a few springs back.
-You did?

305
00:28:40,427 --> 00:28:43,346
-What'd you do?
-Oh, hell. Give me five, too.

306
00:28:43,722 --> 00:28:45,723
Let me ask you something.
How many games you got

307
00:28:45,808 --> 00:28:50,395
-on that field the next couple of weeks?
-Well, we play there next Friday,

308
00:28:50,479 --> 00:28:52,772
but then we're not back
till the end of the month.

309
00:28:52,856 --> 00:28:55,233
You think you can keep your boys
off the field for that length of time?

310
00:28:55,317 --> 00:28:58,444
Now, I'm talking about no practise,
nothing whatsoever.

311
00:28:59,863 --> 00:29:02,407
Henry, why do I get the feeling

312
00:29:02,491 --> 00:29:04,117
this is something
I don't wanna know about?

313
00:29:04,201 --> 00:29:06,077
I'm gonna take back
two of them old ones

314
00:29:06,161 --> 00:29:08,246
-if that's okay with everybody.
-I'll tell you what you do.

315
00:29:08,330 --> 00:29:12,500
You give me three weeks,
and I'll have that field looking greener

316
00:29:12,584 --> 00:29:14,877
than Dublin on Saint Patty's Day.

317
00:29:14,962 --> 00:29:16,754
Fifty cents, fellas.

318
00:29:19,049 --> 00:29:21,092
All right, boys. Let's get a hit.
Come on, boys.

319
00:29:21,176 --> 00:29:23,970
Rudy Bonilla hoping to spark
a Big Lake rally.

320
00:29:24,054 --> 00:29:26,180
Come on, Rudy! Be a hitter!

321
00:29:27,015 --> 00:29:28,766
Keep your eye on it.

322
00:29:31,687 --> 00:29:32,937
Strike three!

323
00:29:33,021 --> 00:29:36,983
Strike three called and the Owls drop
their season opener.

324
00:29:37,985 --> 00:29:39,527
It's all right.

325
00:29:39,778 --> 00:29:41,946
It's okay. Good effort. It's only one.

326
00:29:42,030 --> 00:29:44,699
If we don't start hitting,
it's not gonna be the only one.

327
00:29:44,783 --> 00:29:45,867
Good game, buddy.

328
00:29:45,951 --> 00:29:48,911
Drive safely
on your way home now, folks.

329
00:29:55,002 --> 00:29:58,754
-Where do you keep your brooms, Cal?
-Closet.

330
00:30:20,235 --> 00:30:22,195
Right here, sweetheart.

331
00:30:22,279 --> 00:30:24,530
Cory, keep it right, keep it right now.

332
00:30:33,582 --> 00:30:35,791
-Play's at second, Miguel!
-Two!

333
00:30:42,591 --> 00:30:44,425
-Blue!
-Time!

334
00:30:46,929 --> 00:30:49,472
And the Owls are down ten.

335
00:30:51,850 --> 00:30:55,269
-Just not your day, Rudy.
-Not my year.

336
00:30:58,065 --> 00:31:02,944
Looks like Coach Jimmy Morris
is making a pitching change.

337
00:31:05,489 --> 00:31:07,240
Shake it off, Rudy.

338
00:31:11,036 --> 00:31:15,081
Just a reminder, folks,
the concession stand is still open.

339
00:31:21,797 --> 00:31:24,799
Anybody wanna tell me
how we lost that game?

340
00:31:28,095 --> 00:31:29,262
No?

341
00:31:30,556 --> 00:31:34,725
How about taking a look at the
numbers on that scoreboard out there?

342
00:31:35,477 --> 00:31:37,728
What do those numbers tell you?

343
00:31:38,480 --> 00:31:41,023
How to get a hold of Bo's Tire Barn.

344
00:31:46,530 --> 00:31:47,822
You quit.

345
00:31:50,242 --> 00:31:51,993
You quit out there.

346
00:31:52,953 --> 00:31:56,163
You quit on me.
And worse, you quit on yourselves.

347
00:31:56,999 --> 00:31:58,040
Now, what is it?

348
00:31:58,125 --> 00:32:00,751
You think people don't care
about baseball around here?

349
00:32:00,836 --> 00:32:03,713
Think the school's gonna
drop the programme?

350
00:32:04,339 --> 00:32:06,757
You're just making it easy for them.

351
00:32:06,842 --> 00:32:09,802
Sad part about it
is I see it and you don't.

352
00:32:13,682 --> 00:32:15,766
Look, guys,

353
00:32:15,851 --> 00:32:17,602
most of you,

354
00:32:18,729 --> 00:32:20,855
you're gonna finish up school here.
You're gonna work the rigs.

355
00:32:20,939 --> 00:32:24,191
You're gonna work at Bo's Tire Barn.
You're gonna raise a family and retire.

356
00:32:24,276 --> 00:32:27,570
And you're gonna do all that
right here in Big Lake.

357
00:32:27,904 --> 00:32:30,281
And there's nothing wrong with that.

358
00:32:30,365 --> 00:32:33,075
A lot of real good people
have done that.

359
00:32:33,368 --> 00:32:34,869
I'm doing it.

360
00:32:35,912 --> 00:32:39,332
But if you're looking for
something more after you're done here,

361
00:32:40,417 --> 00:32:43,919
you better give some serious thought
as to how you're gonna play out

362
00:32:44,004 --> 00:32:47,256
-the rest of this season.
-What difference does it make?

363
00:32:47,341 --> 00:32:50,926
I mean, it's not like
any of us are getting scholarships.

364
00:32:51,595 --> 00:32:54,305
I'm not talking about college.

365
00:32:54,389 --> 00:32:57,224
I'm talking about wanting things in life.

366
00:32:58,393 --> 00:33:00,728
I'm talking about having dreams.

367
00:33:02,314 --> 00:33:05,441
And all that starts right here.

368
00:33:05,525 --> 00:33:07,193
Okay? Right here.

369
00:33:08,236 --> 00:33:10,821
You don't have dreams,
you don't have anything.

370
00:33:10,906 --> 00:33:13,240
Coach, what about you?

371
00:33:13,325 --> 00:33:15,785
I mean, you talk about our dreams.

372
00:33:17,746 --> 00:33:19,955
-I mean, I've been catching...
-Joel, we're not talking about me.

373
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:22,083
Yeah, come on, Coach.

374
00:33:22,250 --> 00:33:25,753
Every time you throw,
I gotta ice my hand. Every time.

375
00:33:27,881 --> 00:33:30,591
You're the one who should be
wanting something more.

376
00:33:30,676 --> 00:33:33,177
And the sad part is, I mean,

377
00:33:33,261 --> 00:33:35,262
we see it, and you don't.

378
00:33:36,598 --> 00:33:39,517
I've had my shot, all right? Just...

379
00:33:40,268 --> 00:33:42,978
Look, we got practise tomorrow.

380
00:33:44,439 --> 00:33:47,024
-Let's go.
-So, you take another shot.

381
00:33:47,109 --> 00:33:49,610
-It doesn't work like that.
-It does if you throw hard enough.

382
00:33:49,695 --> 00:33:52,279
I don't throw hard enough. All right?

383
00:33:52,906 --> 00:33:54,532
Now let's hit the showers. Come on.

384
00:33:54,616 --> 00:33:58,119
Now, wait a minute.
We start winning, you try out again.

385
00:34:03,291 --> 00:34:05,751
Last time I checked,
scouts aren't looking

386
00:34:05,836 --> 00:34:07,503
for high school science teachers.

387
00:34:07,587 --> 00:34:10,715
Well, not many science teachers
throw like you.

388
00:34:10,799 --> 00:34:11,882
-Yeah.
-No kidding.

389
00:34:11,967 --> 00:34:13,467
-Yeah.
-No lie.

390
00:34:15,220 --> 00:34:17,888
Take a heck of a lot more
than a couple of wins

391
00:34:17,973 --> 00:34:21,475
-to get me to make a fool of myself.
-All right,

392
00:34:21,560 --> 00:34:23,519
what if we win district?

393
00:34:24,646 --> 00:34:28,149
What if we win district
and go to state playoffs? Then?

394
00:34:31,361 --> 00:34:33,988
-Are you serious?
-Yeah, absolutely.

395
00:34:35,198 --> 00:34:37,366
-Are you serious?
-Yeah.

396
00:34:37,951 --> 00:34:39,118
Yeah.

397
00:34:41,663 --> 00:34:44,665
And all I have to do is just find
some kind of tryout somewhere?

398
00:34:44,750 --> 00:34:46,292
That's it, man.

399
00:34:48,211 --> 00:34:50,254
All right.

400
00:34:50,338 --> 00:34:53,090
-If you win district.
-Yeah!

401
00:34:53,175 --> 00:34:55,468
-Come on, man, we can do this!
-Right on, man.

402
00:34:55,802 --> 00:34:57,344
Yeah, Coach!

403
00:34:59,014 --> 00:35:00,514
We can take it!

404
00:35:03,894 --> 00:35:06,687
I don't get to tell Mom about this, do I?

405
00:38:15,877 --> 00:38:17,461
You sure about this, Henry?

406
00:38:17,545 --> 00:38:21,298
Yeah, deer get a whiff of human hair,
they just keep on moving

407
00:38:21,383 --> 00:38:23,759
till they don't smell it no more.

408
00:38:23,843 --> 00:38:28,681
I figure we put us a circle
all the way around the field here.

409
00:38:29,015 --> 00:38:30,891
That's a lot of hair.

410
00:38:31,601 --> 00:38:35,646
Well, from the looks of things,
contrary to popular opinion,

411
00:38:35,730 --> 00:38:39,233
Cal ain't been cheating nobody
down there at his shop.

412
00:38:44,406 --> 00:38:48,283
It helps if you actually wear
those goggles, Ms Martinez.

413
00:38:49,035 --> 00:38:50,327
Coach.

414
00:38:53,957 --> 00:38:56,667
Yeah, baby. Is that what I think it is?

415
00:38:59,462 --> 00:39:02,506
That, gentlemen,
is Bermuda hybrid number five.

416
00:39:02,674 --> 00:39:05,092
We're gonna have a baseball field.

417
00:39:09,264 --> 00:39:13,434
-Maybe he's not home.
-But he said he'd be here.

418
00:39:17,689 --> 00:39:19,773
-Hey, Grandpa.
-Hunter.

419
00:39:19,858 --> 00:39:24,028
I was reading in the paper this morning
that someone in your family

420
00:39:24,112 --> 00:39:27,740
-is having a birthday.
-That's a kid joke, Grandpa.

421
00:39:27,824 --> 00:39:30,284
Kid joke? No kidding?

422
00:39:33,038 --> 00:39:34,496
-Jimmy.
-Sir.

423
00:39:45,383 --> 00:39:46,967
Whoa.

424
00:39:48,261 --> 00:39:49,845
It's kind of big.

425
00:39:50,138 --> 00:39:53,140
You'll grow into it faster than you think.

426
00:39:53,433 --> 00:39:56,143
Look, Dad, it doesn't have any fingers.

427
00:39:56,811 --> 00:39:59,688
That's 'cause it's a first baseman's mitt.

428
00:40:01,524 --> 00:40:03,067
Is that wrong?

429
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:08,405
Well, you know,
I probably got the receipt somewhere.

430
00:40:08,907 --> 00:40:10,908
We can just,
we can get another one, huh?

431
00:40:10,992 --> 00:40:13,452
-One with fingers.
-That's okay, Grandpa.

432
00:40:13,536 --> 00:40:15,245
I like first base.

433
00:40:18,416 --> 00:40:21,001
Can I go outside and play, Dad?

434
00:40:21,461 --> 00:40:22,544
Yeah.

435
00:40:23,338 --> 00:40:26,090
-What do you say first?
-Thank you, Grandpa.

436
00:40:26,174 --> 00:40:28,509
I really like it a lot.

437
00:40:37,143 --> 00:40:38,769
Stay in the yard.

438
00:40:39,562 --> 00:40:41,897
If he decides
he wants one with fingers...

439
00:40:41,981 --> 00:40:43,774
I'll take care of it.

440
00:40:58,665 --> 00:41:00,791
I didn't know you had these.

441
00:41:01,918 --> 00:41:03,293
Oh, yeah.

442
00:41:04,754 --> 00:41:07,131
Your mother gave me a few.

443
00:41:07,966 --> 00:41:10,175
She'd be the one to have them.

444
00:41:17,392 --> 00:41:25,440
Happy birthday to you

445
00:41:25,859 --> 00:41:30,696
Happy birthday, dear Hunter

446
00:41:31,239 --> 00:41:35,909
Happy birthday to you

447
00:41:38,413 --> 00:41:40,289
-Jessica!
-Hey, don't worry, baby.

448
00:41:40,373 --> 00:41:42,666
Your wish still counts.

449
00:41:42,750 --> 00:41:45,377
Charles? Do you think that,
that ice cream machine

450
00:41:45,461 --> 00:41:47,921
is gonna start spinning itself?

451
00:41:49,507 --> 00:41:51,216
I gotta go get a knife.

452
00:41:51,301 --> 00:41:54,803
-I'll help you, Mom.
-I think we need plates, hon, too.

453
00:42:01,102 --> 00:42:04,897
Seems like five minutes ago I was
watching you blow out those candles.

454
00:42:04,981 --> 00:42:10,527
-Are you trying to make me feel old?
-Come talk to me in 20 years.

455
00:42:13,072 --> 00:42:16,033
Hunter says you spent some time
at your father's today.

456
00:42:16,117 --> 00:42:17,242
Yeah.

457
00:42:18,161 --> 00:42:21,663
-You know where I'm going with that.
-Yes, ma'am, I do.

458
00:42:22,957 --> 00:42:26,251
Lorri says he's trying real hard
to be a good grandfather.

459
00:42:26,336 --> 00:42:28,545
-Yeah, well...
-Lord, Jimmy, I swear,

460
00:42:28,630 --> 00:42:30,964
the one thing you got from him
is his stubborn side.

461
00:42:31,049 --> 00:42:32,883
That's not true.

462
00:42:35,053 --> 00:42:38,972
Well, what do you want me to do?
Pretend everything was perfect?

463
00:42:39,057 --> 00:42:43,393
You've been blaming your father
for too many things for too many years.

464
00:42:44,771 --> 00:42:47,189
I just call them like I see them, Mom.

465
00:42:47,273 --> 00:42:51,318
Jimmy, you can sell that story
someplace else 'cause I ain't buying.

466
00:42:52,195 --> 00:42:54,446
You got your shot at baseball.

467
00:42:54,614 --> 00:42:57,616
You got hurt. Simple as that.

468
00:42:57,700 --> 00:43:00,327
It had nothing to do with your father.

469
00:43:01,329 --> 00:43:03,705
You think he didn't have dreams?

470
00:43:06,334 --> 00:43:09,044
That why it didn't work out
with you two?

471
00:43:09,545 --> 00:43:12,506
I'm gonna need a longer street
for that talk.

472
00:43:17,011 --> 00:43:18,971
Okay, let's get to.

473
00:43:20,014 --> 00:43:21,515
Come on, Wack!

474
00:43:21,766 --> 00:43:23,725
Come on, Coach,
how long we gotta practise here?

475
00:43:23,810 --> 00:43:26,812
-Just a couple of more days.
-Getting old out here, Coach.

476
00:43:26,896 --> 00:43:28,772
Playing in a gravel yard.

477
00:43:28,856 --> 00:43:31,817
Okay, Wack, come on in
for batting practise.

478
00:43:33,528 --> 00:43:35,445
This is not a ball field.

479
00:43:36,197 --> 00:43:38,490
-Come on, let's go.
-Okay, okay.

480
00:43:47,333 --> 00:43:48,542
Okay.

481
00:43:49,210 --> 00:43:53,130
Nice easy swings. Turn your hips.

482
00:43:53,214 --> 00:43:55,465
Throw your hands at the ball.

483
00:43:57,885 --> 00:44:00,345
Come on, Coach.
I mean, if we're gonna start winning,

484
00:44:00,430 --> 00:44:02,639
-we need to see some better pitching.
-Yeah.

485
00:44:02,724 --> 00:44:06,685
-Are you gonna help us or not?
-Come on, Coach, right here.

486
00:44:07,603 --> 00:44:09,688
Yo, let's see some action.

487
00:44:17,071 --> 00:44:18,613
Come on, Wack.

488
00:45:05,286 --> 00:45:06,661
Man!

489
00:45:07,789 --> 00:45:09,539
Hey, right on, man.

490
00:45:10,291 --> 00:45:12,042
Got a piece of that one!

491
00:45:12,126 --> 00:45:13,668
Now,

492
00:45:13,753 --> 00:45:15,712
-get all of it.
-You're the man, Wack.

493
00:45:15,797 --> 00:45:17,506
-Yeah.
-Come on, Wack.

494
00:45:18,174 --> 00:45:19,591
Catching up, Coach.

495
00:45:25,139 --> 00:45:28,809
Go, go, go, go, go!

496
00:45:30,061 --> 00:45:32,145
Attaway, Wack!

497
00:45:32,563 --> 00:45:33,897
Hustle!

498
00:45:37,235 --> 00:45:38,735
Run, boy, run!

499
00:45:47,495 --> 00:45:48,829
Out!

500
00:45:56,087 --> 00:45:57,671
-Take it, Joel!
-Got it!

501
00:45:57,755 --> 00:46:00,132
Run, run, run, run, run, run!

502
00:46:01,759 --> 00:46:02,843
Yeah!

503
00:46:14,230 --> 00:46:18,233
Hit it, Wack! Eye on the ball, buddy!

504
00:46:35,877 --> 00:46:38,712
How about that
for a standing double play?

505
00:46:46,637 --> 00:46:50,891
The Owls win again,
and that is five in a row.

506
00:46:55,229 --> 00:46:56,521
Let's go.

507
00:47:01,068 --> 00:47:02,319
All around! All around!

508
00:47:05,406 --> 00:47:06,907
Strike three!

509
00:47:11,329 --> 00:47:13,246
Yeah!

510
00:47:13,706 --> 00:47:16,917
-Yeah, baby!
-Come on, get some defence going!

511
00:47:20,004 --> 00:47:21,755
He's taking second!

512
00:47:30,014 --> 00:47:31,848
Strike three!

513
00:47:39,398 --> 00:47:42,943
Going, going, gone!

514
00:47:49,992 --> 00:47:52,953
The Owls clear the bases.

515
00:47:59,961 --> 00:48:02,295
-Taking it downtown!
-Yeah!

516
00:48:10,471 --> 00:48:13,473
Hey! Hey! Hey! Keep it down, now,
or folks are gonna think

517
00:48:13,558 --> 00:48:17,519
that football season's started already!

518
00:48:18,980 --> 00:48:22,732
Now, a lot of coaches would say that
they're really proud of you right now.

519
00:48:22,817 --> 00:48:24,317
-Yeah.
-And I am.

520
00:48:26,028 --> 00:48:28,697
A lot of coaches would say

521
00:48:28,781 --> 00:48:33,410
that it's amazing that we won
this many games in a row, and it is.

522
00:48:34,287 --> 00:48:38,290
But! But! But, but, but, but, but,

523
00:48:38,541 --> 00:48:42,127
I am not gonna say that I do not care
about tomorrow's game

524
00:48:42,211 --> 00:48:44,087
'cause I do.

525
00:48:44,171 --> 00:48:46,506
-So let's finish this thing up.
-Yeah!

526
00:48:46,591 --> 00:48:52,304
State! State! State! State! State!
State! State! State! State! State!

527
00:48:52,680 --> 00:48:54,806
-Quite a group you got there.
-Well, thanks.

528
00:48:54,890 --> 00:48:56,349
I'm Steve Dearborn.

529
00:48:56,434 --> 00:48:58,727
I'm with the Barton School District
in Fort Worth.

530
00:48:58,811 --> 00:49:03,231
I was hoping we might be able to talk
about that application you sent us.

531
00:49:03,524 --> 00:49:04,816
Sure.

532
00:49:19,999 --> 00:49:21,708
They'll understand.

533
00:49:22,543 --> 00:49:24,210
I got most of them coming back.

534
00:49:24,295 --> 00:49:26,171
Jimmy, you're always gonna have
kids coming back.

535
00:49:26,255 --> 00:49:27,881
Not these kids.

536
00:49:27,965 --> 00:49:32,552
It's Fort Worth. High school
coaching jobs don't get any better.

537
00:49:33,804 --> 00:49:36,473
You get to coach, you get to teach,

538
00:49:36,557 --> 00:49:39,059
and not that
it's the most important thing in life,

539
00:49:40,353 --> 00:49:42,312
but it's twice the money, Jimmy.

540
00:49:42,396 --> 00:49:44,648
-We sure could use it.
-I know.

541
00:49:47,735 --> 00:49:48,943
Well,

542
00:49:49,820 --> 00:49:54,074
I'm done telling you to sleep on it
because that sure ain't working.

543
00:50:45,960 --> 00:50:47,585
Three years ago,

544
00:50:48,462 --> 00:50:52,716
this programme ended the season
with only one win.

545
00:50:55,761 --> 00:50:57,595
Two years ago,

546
00:50:57,680 --> 00:50:58,972
one win.

547
00:50:59,682 --> 00:51:01,057
Last year,

548
00:51:02,059 --> 00:51:03,476
same story.

549
00:51:05,646 --> 00:51:07,021
This year,

550
00:51:08,274 --> 00:51:10,316
we have won 1 6 games.

551
00:51:14,447 --> 00:51:16,030
Well, gentlemen,

552
00:51:18,993 --> 00:51:20,452
we need 1 7.

553
00:51:23,873 --> 00:51:26,124
Anybody got any questions?

554
00:51:28,043 --> 00:51:29,294
Coach?

555
00:51:31,380 --> 00:51:33,465
You coming back next year?

556
00:51:35,760 --> 00:51:39,846
Wack, why don't we worry about
this year first. All right?

557
00:51:43,934 --> 00:51:46,603
All right, gentlemen, hats off.

558
00:51:51,108 --> 00:51:54,944
Lord, we humbly thank you
for bringing us through this season

559
00:51:55,029 --> 00:51:56,780
without injury and inspiring us...

560
00:51:56,864 --> 00:51:59,824
Were so gallantly streaming

561
00:51:59,909 --> 00:52:04,496
And the rockets' red glare

562
00:52:04,580 --> 00:52:09,000
The bombs bursting in air

563
00:52:09,084 --> 00:52:13,463
Gave proof thro' the night

564
00:52:13,547 --> 00:52:18,301
That our flag was still there

565
00:52:18,385 --> 00:52:25,350
O say, does that star-spangled banner
yet wave

566
00:52:30,231 --> 00:52:36,152
O'er the land of the free

567
00:52:36,237 --> 00:52:43,201
and the home of the brave?

568
00:52:45,663 --> 00:52:51,835
A stirring rendition from Big Lake
Rotary President, Henry Sampson.

569
00:53:04,598 --> 00:53:06,349
All right, all right.

570
00:53:06,433 --> 00:53:08,685
We're a different team
than last time we played these guys.

571
00:53:08,769 --> 00:53:13,398
They're ripe for the picking.
Just let's not be nervous out there.

572
00:53:16,360 --> 00:53:18,945
Nothing to be nervous about.

573
00:53:22,825 --> 00:53:24,784
Let's play baseball.

574
00:53:35,212 --> 00:53:38,089
-Safe!
-Yes. Yeah!

575
00:53:38,173 --> 00:53:40,925
-Come on, guys.
-Guys, come on! Settle down!

576
00:53:41,010 --> 00:53:42,552
Come on! Shake it off, baby!

577
00:53:42,636 --> 00:53:45,430
-Shake it off!
-Just give me the ball!

578
00:53:45,514 --> 00:53:47,098
Damn!

579
00:53:47,182 --> 00:53:50,852
Boys from Big Lake in an early hole.
Two to none.

580
00:53:50,936 --> 00:53:52,645
Okay, keep your heads up.
Keep your heads up.

581
00:53:52,730 --> 00:53:53,938
-Get this thing back to even.
-Come on, boys.

582
00:53:54,023 --> 00:53:55,315
-It's the first inning.
-Okay, guys. It's all right.

583
00:53:55,399 --> 00:53:59,861
All right, Brandon, grab some wood.
Joe David, you're on deck.

584
00:53:59,945 --> 00:54:03,072
This guy's fast ball stinks, so
be looking out for his curve, all right?

585
00:54:03,157 --> 00:54:05,116
-Batter up!
-All right, one hit at a time.

586
00:54:05,200 --> 00:54:08,494
-One swing at a time, one run at a time.
-Come on, now.

587
00:54:08,579 --> 00:54:10,330
Let's go. What do you say, Miguel?

588
00:54:10,414 --> 00:54:12,123
-All right, Miguel. Come on.
-Watch him.

589
00:54:12,207 --> 00:54:14,000
Let's get a hit, baby. Let's get a hit.

590
00:54:17,838 --> 00:54:19,756
He's out!

591
00:54:20,924 --> 00:54:23,635
Owls still with that zero
up on the board.

592
00:54:27,181 --> 00:54:29,932
-Throw him out!
-Out!

593
00:54:30,017 --> 00:54:33,227
A scorcher that dang near
took our boy's head off.

594
00:54:42,613 --> 00:54:44,405
Slide! Slide!

595
00:54:46,659 --> 00:54:49,744
Safe! And that puts Big Lake
in scoring position.

596
00:54:49,828 --> 00:54:51,329
You all right?

597
00:54:53,582 --> 00:54:56,209
Rudy Bonilla coming to the plate.

598
00:55:19,650 --> 00:55:21,442
-Safe! Safe!
-Yeah!

599
00:55:22,069 --> 00:55:25,405
A suicide squeeze.
And the Owls are on the scoreboard.

600
00:55:36,959 --> 00:55:39,085
Come on, ball, go!

601
00:55:42,589 --> 00:55:44,465
Yeah! Yeah!

602
00:55:52,349 --> 00:55:56,102
A spectacular catch,
and the Owls are hanging in there.

603
00:55:57,020 --> 00:55:58,396
Come on, Wack!

604
00:55:58,480 --> 00:56:00,898
Joe David is still on first base.

605
00:56:00,983 --> 00:56:02,734
I see it!
I see the fear in his eyes, Wack!

606
00:56:02,818 --> 00:56:05,361
Come on, baby! Come on, Wack!
Come on! Come on, now!

607
00:56:05,446 --> 00:56:10,491
-Go, Wack! Go, Wack! Go, Wack!
-Come on, Wack!

608
00:56:11,368 --> 00:56:14,662
-Go, Wack!
-Come on, Wack!

609
00:56:31,805 --> 00:56:35,475
-Go! Go! Go!
-Come on!

610
00:56:35,976 --> 00:56:37,643
-Slide, slide!
-Whoa! Whoa!

611
00:56:44,735 --> 00:56:47,445
-Safe!
-Yeah!

612
00:56:48,530 --> 00:56:50,364
Joe David!

613
00:56:52,659 --> 00:56:54,035
Yes!

614
00:56:55,412 --> 00:56:57,914
Big Lake takes the lead.

615
00:57:03,712 --> 00:57:07,173
Rudy Bonilla working with a full count.

616
00:57:07,257 --> 00:57:10,718
Ruffneck runners on first and second.

617
00:57:11,261 --> 00:57:13,387
Come on 'necks! We can do it!

618
00:57:18,393 --> 00:57:20,019
Ball four.

619
00:57:22,481 --> 00:57:26,108
Bases now full of Ruffnecks.

620
00:57:27,611 --> 00:57:29,320
Right here, Rudy.
Right here, right now.

621
00:57:29,404 --> 00:57:30,988
We got him!

622
00:57:31,073 --> 00:57:34,033
Let's go, Rudy. Let's go, Rudy.
Let's get this out.

623
00:57:36,787 --> 00:57:39,413
-Look him down, Rudy.
-Look him down.

624
00:57:40,123 --> 00:57:44,252
Last year's district MVP Cory Jones
coming to the plate.

625
00:57:44,419 --> 00:57:46,087
All right, Cory!

626
00:57:53,262 --> 00:57:56,556
Throw him some heat, Rudy!

627
00:58:05,524 --> 00:58:07,066
Ball!

628
00:58:17,619 --> 00:58:20,580
Come on now, Rudy. Come on, baby!
Get this last out!

629
00:58:20,664 --> 00:58:23,249
-Come on!
-Come on! Strike him out!

630
00:58:24,793 --> 00:58:28,462
-Here we go. Here we go.
-He's got nothing. He's got nothing.

631
00:58:30,173 --> 00:58:32,675
-Look alive!
-Come on! Tighten up now! Tighten up!

632
00:58:38,932 --> 00:58:40,558
Strike!

633
00:58:43,645 --> 00:58:47,815
He is having to dig deep now.
One and one count.

634
00:59:05,918 --> 00:59:08,252
Go. Go.

635
00:59:08,837 --> 00:59:11,088
Foul ball!

636
00:59:11,298 --> 00:59:13,424
Going foul! Yes!

637
00:59:13,508 --> 00:59:14,550
Ball.

638
00:59:14,635 --> 00:59:18,804
Let me tell you, folks, they don't make
foul balls any scarier than that one.

639
00:59:18,889 --> 00:59:21,390
-Come on, Rudy! Let's go!
-Come on, Rudy! You can do it!

640
00:59:21,475 --> 00:59:24,852
Bear down, baby! Bear down!

641
00:59:30,567 --> 00:59:33,110
What do you say, Rudy? Come on!
He can't hit you!

642
00:59:33,195 --> 00:59:35,112
-He ain't got nothing on you!
-He can't hit you!

643
00:59:35,197 --> 00:59:36,656
You gotta get this one, baby! Come on!

644
00:59:36,740 --> 00:59:37,740
Come on, now. Rapid fire, baby.
Rapid fire.

645
00:59:37,824 --> 00:59:39,617
Go, Rudy. It's just a long strike, now.
Here we go.

646
00:59:39,701 --> 00:59:41,369
You can do it, Rudy!
You own him, Rudy!

647
00:59:41,453 --> 00:59:43,704
-Come on, baby! You gotta do this one!
-You own him!

648
00:59:43,789 --> 00:59:45,998
This punk can't hit you, Rudy!
Come on!

649
00:59:46,083 --> 00:59:47,792
-You got him, Rudy!
-He ain't got nothing on you!

650
00:59:47,876 --> 00:59:49,126
Dig deep, Rudy.

651
00:59:49,211 --> 00:59:51,420
One time for all the marbles.
Let's go, baby.

652
00:59:51,505 --> 00:59:54,423
Come on, Rudy! What do you say?
Strike this guy out! Come on!

653
01:00:10,816 --> 01:00:12,692
Strike three!

654
01:00:14,861 --> 01:00:19,907
Owls win! Owls win! Owls win!
Owls win!

655
01:00:59,781 --> 01:01:01,991
Owls win! Owls win!

656
01:01:41,865 --> 01:01:44,283
Hey. Hey, hey, hey, hey!
Settle down, fellas.

657
01:01:44,367 --> 01:01:45,951
Yeah, come on, guys. Quiet down.

658
01:01:46,036 --> 01:01:49,121
To the man who taught us
about wanting something more.

659
01:01:49,206 --> 01:01:53,501
Coach Jimmy Morris of
the District Champion Big Lake Owls!

660
01:01:55,962 --> 01:01:59,048
We wanted it, and now we've got it!

661
01:01:59,382 --> 01:02:01,342
Yeah!

662
01:02:13,021 --> 01:02:15,606
Now it's your turn, Coach.

663
01:02:22,364 --> 01:02:24,573
It's your turn, Coach.

664
01:02:29,496 --> 01:02:31,163
Your turn, Coach.

665
01:02:31,665 --> 01:02:38,921
It's your turn, Coach.

666
01:02:39,881 --> 01:02:43,551
-It's your turn, Coach.
-It's your turn, Coach.

667
01:02:44,261 --> 01:02:48,639
-It's your turn, Coach.
-Your turn, Coach.

668
01:03:19,254 --> 01:03:21,297
Here you go.

669
01:03:21,381 --> 01:03:24,216
Eat your cereal. Come on. Come on.

670
01:03:24,301 --> 01:03:26,510
First day of summer registration.
I'm gonna have to skip breakfast.

671
01:03:26,595 --> 01:03:29,180
Mommy, you can take some
Mister Crunch with you, if you like.

672
01:03:29,264 --> 01:03:31,515
He's a captain, Jessie.
Don't go busting rank on him.

673
01:03:31,600 --> 01:03:34,185
Thanks anyway, hon.

674
01:03:34,269 --> 01:03:35,936
You got anything going today?

675
01:03:37,480 --> 01:03:39,857
Me? No.

676
01:03:39,941 --> 01:03:41,609
We need to go see the real estate guy.

677
01:03:42,485 --> 01:03:43,861
He's been a captain forever.

678
01:03:43,945 --> 01:03:45,362
Why don't they make him
something better?

679
01:03:45,447 --> 01:03:47,698
What you mean, like Admiral Crunch?

680
01:03:47,782 --> 01:03:49,867
Just say, "Yes, ma'am,"
so I know you heard me.

681
01:03:49,951 --> 01:03:53,954
-Yes, ma'am.
-Take them all with you if you go.

682
01:03:56,458 --> 01:03:57,875
You kids mind your daddy.

683
01:03:57,959 --> 01:04:01,003
Remind me not to forget Jamie
if we go anywhere.

684
01:04:29,574 --> 01:04:31,742
They got lots of players, Daddy.

685
01:05:07,404 --> 01:05:09,321
Sign right here.

686
01:05:10,198 --> 01:05:12,825
Have your players get in line, Coach.
Everybody signs in.

687
01:05:12,909 --> 01:05:16,453
-Jim Morris.
-Dave Patterson.

688
01:05:16,538 --> 01:05:18,289
I saw you
when the Brewers drafted you.

689
01:05:18,373 --> 01:05:19,832
-Yeah.
-Must have been...

690
01:05:19,916 --> 01:05:21,959
-A while back.
-Yeah.

691
01:05:22,043 --> 01:05:24,253
-How you doing, Dave?
-Good.

692
01:05:24,337 --> 01:05:27,548
You still looking at players?

693
01:05:27,841 --> 01:05:31,593
Well, the looking's the easy part.
It's the finding that gives me trouble.

694
01:05:31,678 --> 01:05:34,888
You bringing some kids?
Besides your own?

695
01:05:36,474 --> 01:05:39,518
-I'm here for me.
-For you?

696
01:05:41,021 --> 01:05:43,439
Well, all right, then.

697
01:05:43,523 --> 01:05:46,025
-Good to see you.
-It's good. Good to see you.

698
01:05:46,109 --> 01:05:47,526
Sign here.

699
01:06:00,790 --> 01:06:05,419
Come on. Let's go. Excuse me.

700
01:06:37,786 --> 01:06:41,121
-Daddy, you can be X's this time.
-Okay.

701
01:06:59,724 --> 01:07:01,392
Now, now.

702
01:07:03,061 --> 01:07:04,686
That's the last diaper.

703
01:07:04,771 --> 01:07:07,815
Well, it's okay. We're outta here just
as soon as Jamie's set to go.

704
01:07:07,899 --> 01:07:10,401
-But, Dad.
-No "But, Dads," okay?

705
01:07:10,485 --> 01:07:13,195
I'm dealing
with enough butts here as it is.

706
01:07:14,155 --> 01:07:16,698
Hold on, honey. Daddy's almost done.

707
01:07:19,619 --> 01:07:23,539
Hunter, look, I promised to try out.

708
01:07:23,623 --> 01:07:26,792
There's nothing I can do
if they won't take a look at me.

709
01:07:26,876 --> 01:07:30,504
Hey, Jimmy! Come on, you're up.

710
01:07:33,758 --> 01:07:36,093
Yeah. Just a second!

711
01:07:37,762 --> 01:07:39,847
I'm not doing it.

712
01:07:40,557 --> 01:07:43,642
All right.
Look, look, you just stay right here,

713
01:07:43,726 --> 01:07:45,686
and you look after your sister, all right?

714
01:07:45,770 --> 01:07:47,438
Come on. Let's go.

715
01:07:53,486 --> 01:07:55,237
Sit!

716
01:07:58,533 --> 01:07:59,867
Be right back.

717
01:08:06,207 --> 01:08:08,959
Who do we got here? All right. All right.

718
01:08:13,882 --> 01:08:17,885
-You need to warm up?
-No, I'm good.

719
01:08:18,011 --> 01:08:21,138
All right. Don't hurt yourself.

720
01:08:26,561 --> 01:08:29,271
Whenever you're ready!

721
01:08:32,150 --> 01:08:34,568
Whenever I'm ready.

722
01:09:36,422 --> 01:09:39,466
-You want me to keep going?
-Couple more!

723
01:10:35,565 --> 01:10:37,733
That's good, Jim.

724
01:10:45,199 --> 01:10:47,492
Pitch. Pitch.

725
01:10:47,577 --> 01:10:49,453
Man, you were bringing
some heat out there.

726
01:10:49,537 --> 01:10:52,080
-Come on.
-You had them talking.

727
01:10:52,373 --> 01:10:55,792
You had them talking. Good job, man.

728
01:10:56,919 --> 01:10:58,754
That was Jessica.

729
01:11:02,759 --> 01:11:06,094
-Jim.
-You did good though, man.

730
01:11:06,179 --> 01:11:09,222
How fast were you throwing
1 5 years ago?

731
01:11:11,184 --> 01:11:13,852
Slow enough that scouts
stopped using the word fast.

732
01:11:13,936 --> 01:11:19,733
-Jim, how fast were you throwing?
-I don't know. 85, 86. Why?

733
01:11:19,817 --> 01:11:24,488
-You just threw 98 miles an hour.
-No.

734
01:11:24,572 --> 01:11:27,574
Dozen straight pitches.
Three radar guns.

735
01:11:27,659 --> 01:11:30,827
-Same thing on all of them.
-No, Dave, there's no way that I was...

736
01:11:30,912 --> 01:11:33,246
Now, I been a scout a long time.

737
01:11:33,331 --> 01:11:36,667
And rule number one is
arms slow down when they get old.

738
01:11:36,751 --> 01:11:38,502
Listen, Jim, I call the office
and I tell them

739
01:11:38,586 --> 01:11:41,672
I got a guy here almost twice
these kids' age,

740
01:11:41,756 --> 01:11:42,923
I'm gonna get laughed at.

741
01:11:43,007 --> 01:11:46,760
But if I don't call in a 98 mile an hour
fast ball, I'm gonna get fired.

742
01:11:46,844 --> 01:11:50,597
I'm just letting you know that there's
a chance you might get a call on this.

743
01:11:55,228 --> 01:12:00,190
You figure out what I saw out there
today, you let me know. Okay?

744
01:12:01,109 --> 01:12:02,734
Okay.

745
01:12:34,809 --> 01:12:37,185
Hey, hon, don't fix dinner.
We're getting some pizza.

746
01:12:37,270 --> 01:12:38,895
Oh, thank God.

747
01:12:38,980 --> 01:12:42,065
Jim, this is Dan Jordan with
the Tampa Bay Devil Rays.

748
01:12:42,150 --> 01:12:44,151
l'll try you back later.

749
01:12:45,278 --> 01:12:48,447
Jim, Mark Rafus with
the Tampa Bay Devil Rays.

750
01:12:48,531 --> 01:12:50,490
Dave Patterson told me about
this afternoon.

751
01:12:50,575 --> 01:12:52,784
l'll try you first thing in the morning.

752
01:12:53,786 --> 01:12:56,663
Hey, Jim, this is Dave. Listen, we're
gonna have you throw again

753
01:12:56,748 --> 01:12:57,914
in a couple of days just to make sure.

754
01:12:57,999 --> 01:12:59,833
They're gonna send out
some of the boys from Tampa.

755
01:13:06,799 --> 01:13:09,176
Mommy!

756
01:13:12,805 --> 01:13:16,641
Daddy told me to give you the pizza
and not say anything else.

757
01:13:17,435 --> 01:13:23,148
Thank you, sweet pea.
I'm sure that's exactly what he told you.

758
01:13:24,859 --> 01:13:27,444
All right, all right. Go, go ahead.

759
01:13:30,072 --> 01:13:32,616
There was like a thousand players,
and it was so hot.

760
01:13:32,700 --> 01:13:34,451
And Daddy was pitching really hard.

761
01:13:34,535 --> 01:13:35,744
-Oh, yeah?
-Yeah.

762
01:13:35,870 --> 01:13:38,121
-And it was really hot, but it was fun.
-Yeah.

763
01:13:38,206 --> 01:13:40,332
-And I got my nose burned really bad.
-Yeah.

764
01:13:42,376 --> 01:13:45,462
The guy thought
that the damn radar gun was broken.

765
01:13:46,589 --> 01:13:51,176
Do you know how many guys
can throw the ball 98 miles an hour?

766
01:13:51,260 --> 01:13:54,304
-Not many?
-You can count them on one hand.

767
01:13:54,388 --> 01:13:56,515
I still don't believe it. I mean...

768
01:13:56,599 --> 01:13:59,059
Those are major league scouts
on our message machine.

769
01:13:59,143 --> 01:14:01,895
I know. It's great.

770
01:14:04,857 --> 01:14:07,442
Listen, honey, why didn't you tell me
about all this?

771
01:14:07,527 --> 01:14:11,029
-Well, I just thought you'd laugh at me.
-Oh, come on.

772
01:14:11,113 --> 01:14:15,450
It was just... It was just this... It was this
thing to get the kids to start playing.

773
01:14:15,535 --> 01:14:18,870
I thought I'd just go there, I'd throw
a few pitches, then I'd be done with it.

774
01:14:28,172 --> 01:14:32,551
-So you're considering this?
-I don't know.

775
01:14:35,054 --> 01:14:40,684
I don't know.
I have never thrown that hard before.

776
01:14:45,606 --> 01:14:47,232
What?

777
01:14:50,278 --> 01:14:52,237
You don't seem too excited.

778
01:14:52,321 --> 01:14:55,532
No, I am. Really.

779
01:14:59,453 --> 01:15:02,873
lt's a tough decision. Do you go ahead
and call in your setup man

780
01:15:02,957 --> 01:15:06,293
to try to counter the left-handed batter
with the lefty middle reliever?

781
01:15:06,460 --> 01:15:09,713
That's right, Tom. They have Johnson
available as well as Bisou,

782
01:15:09,797 --> 01:15:12,465
who did work one full inning
on Tuesday night.

783
01:15:12,550 --> 01:15:14,426
Yup, now it appears
that they've made up their minds

784
01:15:14,510 --> 01:15:16,177
and they're gonna go the setup route.

785
01:15:16,262 --> 01:15:17,721
And here comes Grey
out of the bullpen,

786
01:15:17,805 --> 01:15:20,891
making the traditional run
through the outfield to the mound.

787
01:15:20,975 --> 01:15:24,102
You know, Jim, l'm sure no matter
how many times you've made that run,

788
01:15:24,186 --> 01:15:26,730
it still has to be quite a thrill.

789
01:15:26,814 --> 01:15:29,441
Right you are, Jim, back to the action.

790
01:16:16,113 --> 01:16:18,949
Ain't nothing wrong with that
one right there.

791
01:16:36,300 --> 01:16:38,969
So much for his arm falling off, huh?

792
01:17:18,551 --> 01:17:21,011
-Jimmy?
-Yeah. Yes, sir.

793
01:17:21,095 --> 01:17:25,598
I was just driving by.
I saw that your light was on.

794
01:17:25,683 --> 01:17:28,351
It'll be on for a few more hours.

795
01:17:29,729 --> 01:17:31,354
-You want to come in?
-No, that's all right.

796
01:17:31,439 --> 01:17:34,816
I was... I got to be heading back.

797
01:17:35,484 --> 01:17:38,111
I just wanted to...

798
01:17:40,448 --> 01:17:43,366
-Lorri called.
-Yeah?

799
01:17:43,451 --> 01:17:46,286
Guess the scouts
saw what they wanted to see.

800
01:17:47,913 --> 01:17:50,832
Well, I don't know what to do.

801
01:17:52,334 --> 01:17:54,919
Give it some time.

802
01:17:55,421 --> 01:17:58,423
I haven't got a lot of that.

803
01:18:01,177 --> 01:18:03,928
You're asking me?

804
01:18:07,308 --> 01:18:09,100
Yes, sir.

805
01:18:15,816 --> 01:18:19,486
Your grandfather once told me
it was okay

806
01:18:19,612 --> 01:18:22,906
to think about what you want to do,

807
01:18:22,990 --> 01:18:26,493
until it was time to start doing what
you were meant to do.

808
01:18:28,662 --> 01:18:31,498
That may not be
what you wanted to hear.

809
01:18:48,349 --> 01:18:51,017
-Good night, Jimmy.
-Good night.

810
01:19:07,785 --> 01:19:10,453
I swear, sometimes I think
he lies in bed at night

811
01:19:10,538 --> 01:19:14,374
just figuring out the one thing
that he can say that hurts the most.

812
01:19:14,458 --> 01:19:18,002
I was this close to thinking he was
gonna give me some good advice.

813
01:19:18,712 --> 01:19:20,713
Maybe he did.

814
01:19:21,674 --> 01:19:23,007
What?

815
01:19:23,926 --> 01:19:28,012
Oh, boy. Listen, Jimmy, I love you.

816
01:19:28,139 --> 01:19:29,764
Lord knows, I think it's great
you're going out there

817
01:19:29,849 --> 01:19:31,808
and showing up the kids at the try out,

818
01:19:31,892 --> 01:19:34,561
but I'm a little concerned that
you might be losing sight of the fact

819
01:19:34,645 --> 01:19:36,062
you have a family who needs you

820
01:19:36,147 --> 01:19:39,023
and a pretty decent job
waiting for you up at Fort Worth.

821
01:19:39,108 --> 01:19:41,818
For the record, all right,
I never said I was gonna do this.

822
01:19:41,902 --> 01:19:43,069
Come on, Jimmy. I know you.

823
01:19:43,154 --> 01:19:46,364
Although I was hoping for a little bit
more support on the home front.

824
01:19:46,448 --> 01:19:49,200
So this doesn't concern me or the kids?

825
01:19:49,285 --> 01:19:51,035
You can't eat dreams, Jimmy.

826
01:19:51,120 --> 01:19:54,581
And they don't pay for clothes or shoes
or gas or babysitters.

827
01:19:54,665 --> 01:19:56,416
Now, I do not want
to be the bad guy on this,

828
01:19:56,500 --> 01:19:58,168
but somebody around here
has got to start being rational.

829
01:19:58,252 --> 01:20:00,670
You know what?
That's all I've been my whole life!

830
01:20:00,754 --> 01:20:05,508
Yeah, what about my life?
I was there, too, remember?

831
01:20:05,593 --> 01:20:07,927
Every time you got hurt,
I got a front row seat

832
01:20:08,012 --> 01:20:11,264
to watch you shut yourself off
from the rest of the world

833
01:20:11,348 --> 01:20:13,141
and from me.

834
01:20:16,604 --> 01:20:19,272
Truth is, I was happy when you quit.

835
01:20:22,776 --> 01:20:24,402
Happy.

836
01:20:26,780 --> 01:20:27,947
Yeah.

837
01:20:29,783 --> 01:20:32,827
Because I don't wanna
see you get hurt again.

838
01:22:12,636 --> 01:22:16,014
-Kids down?
-For a while at least.

839
01:22:20,060 --> 01:22:22,228
I'm sorry.

840
01:22:22,313 --> 01:22:24,397
Yeah, me too.

841
01:22:27,818 --> 01:22:30,403
-I've been thinking...
-So have I. I think you should do this.

842
01:22:30,487 --> 01:22:33,614
-No. No. You were right.
-No.

843
01:22:34,241 --> 01:22:35,992
I don't think so.

844
01:22:41,498 --> 01:22:44,876
We've got an eight-year-old boy
inside this house

845
01:22:44,960 --> 01:22:47,003
who waited all day
in the sun and the rain

846
01:22:47,087 --> 01:22:51,090
to see his daddy try to do something
that nobody believed he could do.

847
01:22:52,468 --> 01:22:57,930
Now, what are we telling him
if you don't try now?

848
01:23:09,526 --> 01:23:12,862
I can't leave you here with all this.

849
01:23:12,946 --> 01:23:14,739
Jimmy Morris, I'm a Texas woman,

850
01:23:14,823 --> 01:23:18,910
which means I don't need the help
of a man to keep things running.

851
01:23:21,622 --> 01:23:23,206
Okay?

852
01:23:33,967 --> 01:23:41,432
Okay.

853
01:23:41,517 --> 01:23:42,725
Great.

854
01:24:26,103 --> 01:24:29,188
See you soon, little man.

855
01:25:41,011 --> 01:25:44,764
Excuse me. Excuse me.

856
01:25:49,686 --> 01:25:53,022
-You the old guy?
-I'm the old guy.

857
01:25:55,108 --> 01:25:57,610
Come on, Rays! You're on a roll!

858
01:25:59,112 --> 01:26:00,905
Hey, big time.

859
01:26:00,989 --> 01:26:04,242
You use a walker to get to the mound,
or do the other players carry you?

860
01:26:04,326 --> 01:26:07,495
Hey, pitch, who'd you come up with?
The Senators?

861
01:26:07,579 --> 01:26:11,541
What are they paying you?
They ought to pay us to come see you!

862
01:26:11,625 --> 01:26:13,125
Welcome to last place!

863
01:26:15,754 --> 01:26:19,423
Hey, skipper! I didn't know it was
Bring Your Dad To Work night.

864
01:26:26,390 --> 01:26:27,890
Way to bring up his average!

865
01:26:27,975 --> 01:26:29,058
Time!

866
01:26:29,142 --> 01:26:31,394
Pitching change for Orlando.

867
01:26:31,478 --> 01:26:36,482
Now pitching, number nine,
Johnny Morris.

868
01:26:36,567 --> 01:26:38,901
Check that, Jimmy Morris.

869
01:26:38,986 --> 01:26:42,947
And now, fans,
it's time to guess the winning car!

870
01:26:43,031 --> 01:26:46,367
And a big crash at the first base line!

871
01:26:46,660 --> 01:26:49,412
Number one will win it this time.

872
01:26:49,496 --> 01:26:51,581
Who is this guy?

873
01:26:51,665 --> 01:26:54,125
Work fast. We got a long bus trip.

874
01:26:54,626 --> 01:26:57,795
Let's go, Stingrays!

875
01:27:24,865 --> 01:27:29,827
Hey, pitch!
Do I look like a strike zone to you?

876
01:27:30,370 --> 01:27:31,704
You stink!

877
01:27:33,415 --> 01:27:35,750
l can't believe they let
you pitch your first game.

878
01:27:35,834 --> 01:27:38,377
-Well, how'd you do?
-Not bad.

879
01:27:40,881 --> 01:27:43,090
I had a few of them get away from me.

880
01:27:43,175 --> 01:27:44,508
Did the fans all yell when you ran in?

881
01:27:44,593 --> 01:27:47,136
Yeah. Oh, yeah,
they were yelling, all right.

882
01:27:47,471 --> 01:27:50,181
Honey, that's just great.
Hey, let me get the kids.

883
01:27:50,265 --> 01:27:51,807
No.

884
01:27:51,892 --> 01:27:55,770
Just tell them l'll call them tomorrow
from wherever we're going.

885
01:27:55,854 --> 01:27:59,065
Okay. I will.

886
01:28:01,777 --> 01:28:03,235
I love you.

887
01:28:05,989 --> 01:28:09,742
-I love you, too.
-Take care.

888
01:29:50,844 --> 01:29:52,052
Blue!

889
01:29:54,264 --> 01:29:57,349
-Okay, what's four times four?
-Seventeen?

890
01:29:57,434 --> 01:30:02,271
No, it's not seventeen.
You're guessing. Just add it up.

891
01:30:03,440 --> 01:30:05,357
-Sixteen?
-lt's sixteen.

892
01:30:05,442 --> 01:30:07,610
You gotta be able to look
at four times four,

893
01:30:07,694 --> 01:30:10,654
and just know that it's sixteen
without even having to think about it.

894
01:30:10,739 --> 01:30:12,072
All right?

895
01:30:12,157 --> 01:30:15,159
-Now, let's do your fives.
-Daddy, l already done my fives.

896
01:30:15,243 --> 01:30:17,203
What's...

897
01:30:21,541 --> 01:30:22,958
Well, if you want to learn
how to multiply,

898
01:30:23,043 --> 01:30:24,502
you're gonna have to practise, bud.

899
01:30:24,586 --> 01:30:26,170
Okay.

900
01:30:26,254 --> 01:30:28,005
-Hey, Dad.
-Yeah, partner?

901
01:30:28,089 --> 01:30:30,966
Told you when you're going
to the big leagues yet?

902
01:30:33,428 --> 01:30:36,639
-Could you put your mom on? Okay?
-Okay.

903
01:30:36,723 --> 01:30:38,057
All right.

904
01:30:42,562 --> 01:30:44,897
Thanks, hon.

905
01:30:44,981 --> 01:30:46,148
Hey.

906
01:30:46,233 --> 01:30:48,526
Jessie says they left you in
one too many pitches.

907
01:30:48,610 --> 01:30:52,822
Jessie needs to learn
how to keep a secret with her father.

908
01:30:52,906 --> 01:30:55,199
-How are things going?
-Things are good.

909
01:30:55,283 --> 01:30:57,910
Hunter's got a field trip on Friday.
And the rest of us are talking about...

910
01:30:57,994 --> 01:30:59,870
Honey, that's not what I meant.

911
01:30:59,955 --> 01:31:05,000
I know. It's not bad.
We're a little behind.

912
01:31:05,085 --> 01:31:06,293
How much?

913
01:31:06,378 --> 01:31:09,797
Well, they took the bed,
but they left the nightstand.

914
01:31:14,928 --> 01:31:19,098
-You all right?
-l'm just tired.

915
01:31:19,182 --> 01:31:21,267
-I love you.
-Love you, too.

916
01:31:21,393 --> 01:31:23,853
-Good night. Good night.
-Okay. Good night.

917
01:31:32,279 --> 01:31:33,279
Listen, man,

918
01:31:33,363 --> 01:31:35,865
this is the only long distance phone
we have here at the Ritz.

919
01:31:35,949 --> 01:31:39,952
You see someone waiting,
you keep it to1 0 minutes, okay?

920
01:31:42,122 --> 01:31:45,207
What? Now you all talked out?

921
01:31:46,459 --> 01:31:49,128
Use it all up on your girlfriend?

922
01:31:51,298 --> 01:31:55,509
It was my son.
He needed help with his homework.

923
01:32:06,021 --> 01:32:07,688
Get out of here.

924
01:32:08,607 --> 01:32:11,984
It looks like a little screen time's
coming for Kenny Justin.

925
01:32:16,656 --> 01:32:20,326
Jim! Hey Jim, come here a minute.

926
01:32:24,581 --> 01:32:26,916
Jim, I want you to meet
Tim Stewart from ABC News.

927
01:32:27,000 --> 01:32:28,000
-Hey, Jim.
-It's a pleasure.

928
01:32:28,084 --> 01:32:30,586
Thanks for taking some time with us.
You want to sit down?

929
01:32:30,670 --> 01:32:32,504
Got a jersey for you.

930
01:32:33,548 --> 01:32:35,299
Can you believe that?

931
01:32:35,383 --> 01:32:37,468
I go four for five last night
and look where they got the camera.

932
01:32:37,552 --> 01:32:40,054
Man's been pitching good enough
to talk about it.

933
01:32:40,138 --> 01:32:43,307
Dude's old enough.
He needs to talk about retiring.

934
01:33:17,300 --> 01:33:19,259
Come on, let's go.

935
01:33:23,306 --> 01:33:25,057
Grab my magazine.

936
01:33:37,028 --> 01:33:39,822
Old Man River,
you're moving kind of slow.

937
01:33:39,906 --> 01:33:42,533
Yeah, six hours on a bus.

938
01:33:42,659 --> 01:33:45,494
-Yeah?
-Never mind.

939
01:33:45,578 --> 01:33:48,706
Come on, man. Look, I got just
the recipe to loosen them bones up.

940
01:33:55,964 --> 01:33:57,381
Here you go.

941
01:34:01,302 --> 01:34:05,139
Gentlemen, to the diet that put
Babe Ruth in the Hall Of Fame.

942
01:34:05,223 --> 01:34:08,142
Hey, River, what was it like
watching the Babe play?

943
01:34:09,019 --> 01:34:11,520
Okay. You sure you wanna start this?

944
01:34:11,604 --> 01:34:14,606
How many fans did you guys lose when
you raised ticket prices to 50 cents?

945
01:34:14,691 --> 01:34:17,067
Yeah. Not as many as we lose
when you pitch.

946
01:34:18,361 --> 01:34:20,070
And this game is over, baby.

947
01:34:20,155 --> 01:34:24,033
River, I swear, if you were this funny
all the time, nobody'd say nothing...

948
01:34:27,162 --> 01:34:29,121
About what?

949
01:34:31,458 --> 01:34:33,167
What do you mean?

950
01:34:33,960 --> 01:34:36,795
What are they saying, Brooks?

951
01:34:36,963 --> 01:34:38,881
Look, some of the guys figure,
you know,

952
01:34:38,965 --> 01:34:41,925
the way the team's
pushing your story and all, that...

953
01:34:42,469 --> 01:34:45,888
What, do they think
I'm some kind of publicity stunt?

954
01:34:46,639 --> 01:34:50,392
Is that what they're thinking?
They think I'm taking somebody's spot.

955
01:34:52,979 --> 01:34:56,190
What do you think, Brooks?

956
01:34:57,150 --> 01:35:00,611
You're too fast for me. That's all I know.

957
01:35:07,827 --> 01:35:09,995
Go, Brooksie! That's in the gap!

958
01:35:10,080 --> 01:35:11,080
Go, go, go, go, go!

959
01:35:11,164 --> 01:35:13,707
-All the way, Brooks!
-Come on, Brooks!

960
01:35:14,918 --> 01:35:18,003
Safe! Time!

961
01:35:18,171 --> 01:35:20,547
-You wanna warm up?
-Nice job, Brooks!

962
01:35:20,632 --> 01:35:22,591
-Yeah.
-Don't expect a raise, Brooks!

963
01:35:22,717 --> 01:35:24,093
Peanuts!

964
01:35:26,679 --> 01:35:33,018
Now batting for the Bulls,
number 38, Kenny Bryerson.

965
01:35:52,831 --> 01:35:56,542
Mac, we both know
it's gonna be Brooks.

966
01:35:56,709 --> 01:35:59,628
I've had more than one player
called up before.

967
01:35:59,712 --> 01:36:01,421
Anybody my age?

968
01:36:03,758 --> 01:36:05,342
So, what are you thinking?

969
01:36:05,426 --> 01:36:10,180
I got a pile of unpaid bills at home,
I got a good job there,

970
01:36:10,265 --> 01:36:12,558
I only make $600 a month here

971
01:36:12,642 --> 01:36:15,811
and I got a family
I haven't seen in three months.

972
01:36:15,895 --> 01:36:17,855
We still got some season left.

973
01:36:21,734 --> 01:36:25,404
Somebody once told me that it's okay
to think about what you want to do

974
01:36:25,488 --> 01:36:29,074
until it's time to start doing
what you were meant to do.

975
01:36:31,119 --> 01:36:34,746
Just so you know, you were
my best relief pitcher this past month.

976
01:36:41,254 --> 01:36:43,922
Thanks, Mac.

977
01:36:52,557 --> 01:36:55,726
-Jessica, give me that back! It's mine!
-No, Hunter!

978
01:36:55,852 --> 01:36:59,021
I'm gonna tell Mommy! Come on.

979
01:36:59,105 --> 01:37:00,522
-Mine. Mine. It's always been mine.
-Jamie's asleep.

980
01:37:00,607 --> 01:37:03,150
-You're gonna be in big trouble.
-Hello?

981
01:37:03,234 --> 01:37:05,110
-Hey.
-Hey!

982
01:37:05,236 --> 01:37:07,946
I didn't think
I'd hear from you till tomorrow.

983
01:37:08,990 --> 01:37:10,616
l'm coming home.

984
01:37:13,161 --> 01:37:15,162
-What? Did you get hurt?
-No.

985
01:37:15,246 --> 01:37:19,625
I'm fine. It's just time.

986
01:37:19,751 --> 01:37:26,381
-You called Fort Worth, didn't you?
-I told them I'd be there in two weeks.

987
01:37:27,300 --> 01:37:32,846
-So pitch for two weeks.
-No, there's too much to do.

988
01:37:32,931 --> 01:37:34,389
I'll take care of it.

989
01:37:34,474 --> 01:37:35,515
We've been okay for three months.

990
01:37:35,600 --> 01:37:37,976
-Lorri? Lorri?
-We'll be okay for two weeks.

991
01:37:40,647 --> 01:37:43,815
No, I'm wasting my time out here.

992
01:37:45,276 --> 01:37:46,735
Listen, Jimmy.

993
01:37:46,819 --> 01:37:50,239
Lord knows, I am ready for both sides
of the bed to be warm again,

994
01:37:50,907 --> 01:37:51,990
but if you're gonna do this,

995
01:37:52,158 --> 01:37:53,992
you make sure you're doing it
for the right reasons.

996
01:37:54,077 --> 01:37:57,579
Because you're the one
that's gonna have to live with it, okay?

997
01:37:57,664 --> 01:38:00,999
-I'll be fine.
-You still love it?

998
01:38:05,922 --> 01:38:07,673
Just think about that, okay?

999
01:38:16,849 --> 01:38:19,101
Finally tonight, dreams that don't die.

1000
01:38:19,185 --> 01:38:22,187
ln 1983, the Milwaukee Brewers
drafted a pitcher named Jim Morris.

1001
01:38:22,272 --> 01:38:24,273
You've never heard of him.
Arm troubles.

1002
01:38:24,357 --> 01:38:27,109
He quickly dropped out of baseball,
married, had kids,

1003
01:38:27,193 --> 01:38:29,361
taught high school chemistry.

1004
01:38:29,445 --> 01:38:32,447
Pitching batting practise
to the high school kids,

1005
01:38:32,532 --> 01:38:35,492
he found he was throwing the ball
95 miles an hour.

1006
01:38:35,660 --> 01:38:38,370
Faster than he could throw it
years ago.

1007
01:38:38,830 --> 01:38:42,749
He doesn't know how that happened.
But guess what it did to his dream?

1008
01:38:42,834 --> 01:38:44,126
Here's Aaron Brown.

1009
01:38:44,210 --> 01:38:46,878
Jim Morris is afraid to tell his mother

1010
01:38:46,963 --> 01:38:51,133
he quit his old job as
a high school teacher for his new one,

1011
01:38:51,217 --> 01:38:53,635
a relief pitcher in the minor leagues.

1012
01:38:53,720 --> 01:38:56,763
He is really old,
too old to be in the minors,

1013
01:38:56,848 --> 01:38:59,141
but he's also throwing
harder and better

1014
01:38:59,225 --> 01:39:01,435
than he did as a 20-year-old kid.

1015
01:39:01,519 --> 01:39:03,854
And so he's back
in that strange and wonderful land

1016
01:39:03,938 --> 01:39:05,355
called minor league baseball,

1017
01:39:05,440 --> 01:39:08,150
where the Bull snorts smoke
on every home run,

1018
01:39:08,234 --> 01:39:11,278
and future stars
run the bases between innings.

1019
01:39:11,404 --> 01:39:14,614
l made a challenge to the guys
to follow their dreams.

1020
01:39:14,699 --> 01:39:16,867
Really, it was something
to motivate them,

1021
01:39:16,951 --> 01:39:19,745
and they turned it around on me

1022
01:39:19,829 --> 01:39:23,165
and told me
l wasn't following my own dreams.

1023
01:39:23,249 --> 01:39:25,500
-They are a great bunch of guys.
-Be quiet. I'm trying to watch.

1024
01:39:25,585 --> 01:39:27,627
The town has been very supportive
of me.

1025
01:39:27,712 --> 01:39:29,338
l grew up here.

1026
01:39:29,422 --> 01:39:32,883
l've had a lot of really good support
from my family and friends

1027
01:39:32,967 --> 01:39:35,344
and townspeople around there.

1028
01:39:35,428 --> 01:39:38,930
This game, it's been
the true love of my life,

1029
01:39:39,015 --> 01:39:43,226
other than my wife, Lorri,
since l was a little boy.

1030
01:39:43,311 --> 01:39:48,023
l had a dream as a little boy
of playing professional baseball.

1031
01:39:48,107 --> 01:39:50,359
l used to pitch and imagine myself,
you know,

1032
01:39:50,443 --> 01:39:54,029
like every other kid in this country,
of being in the World Series,

1033
01:39:54,113 --> 01:39:58,575
pitching in the seventh game,
ninth inning, big league ballpark.

1034
01:39:58,659 --> 01:40:02,954
There's drama in it. There's suspense.

1035
01:40:03,039 --> 01:40:07,167
There's a camaraderie with the
other players, the fans in the stadium.

1036
01:40:07,251 --> 01:40:11,463
You add that to it. lt's a great game.

1037
01:40:45,289 --> 01:40:47,666
Heads up out there. Heads up.

1038
01:40:49,877 --> 01:40:52,295
Energy up, now. Come on, boys!

1039
01:40:59,429 --> 01:41:02,389
Ready to play!

1040
01:41:18,072 --> 01:41:20,157
-Riv!
-Hey.

1041
01:41:23,453 --> 01:41:26,121
You know what we get to do today,
Brooks?

1042
01:41:26,205 --> 01:41:28,331
We get to play baseball.

1043
01:41:30,209 --> 01:41:31,334
Two!

1044
01:41:38,134 --> 01:41:42,345
O-and-two the count on Williams.
Morris really on top of his game.

1045
01:41:42,847 --> 01:41:44,598
Coach is really bringing it tonight.

1046
01:41:48,644 --> 01:41:51,688
Called strike three.
Caught him looking with a fast ball.

1047
01:41:51,772 --> 01:41:54,649
That's Morris' third strikeout
of the evening.

1048
01:42:02,533 --> 01:42:06,077
-Infield! Infield!
-I got it! I got it!

1049
01:42:21,260 --> 01:42:22,928
Thanks for cleaning up
my mess tonight.

1050
01:42:23,012 --> 01:42:26,264
Hey, you pitched seven good innings.
I wouldn't call that a mess.

1051
01:42:29,769 --> 01:42:31,269
Jimmy.

1052
01:42:40,530 --> 01:42:42,614
Go ahead and close the door.

1053
01:42:47,620 --> 01:42:51,039
Jimmy, I just got off the phone
with the big club.

1054
01:42:52,625 --> 01:42:55,001
They're calling up Brooks.

1055
01:42:56,671 --> 01:42:59,172
That's great.

1056
01:42:59,257 --> 01:43:00,632
He won't be back.

1057
01:43:00,716 --> 01:43:02,926
Well, he respects you

1058
01:43:03,135 --> 01:43:06,596
and I thought you might want
to be the one to tell him.

1059
01:43:09,517 --> 01:43:12,519
-Sure.
-Being as that you're going, too.

1060
01:43:19,193 --> 01:43:21,486
They're calling me up?

1061
01:43:23,823 --> 01:43:27,117
Two of you fly out of here tonight.
You catch up with the team tomorrow.

1062
01:43:30,955 --> 01:43:33,832
-Where?
-Well, they're on the road right now.

1063
01:43:35,293 --> 01:43:36,876
In Texas.

1064
01:44:12,663 --> 01:44:14,664
-Hello?
-Hey, Lorri.

1065
01:44:14,749 --> 01:44:17,500
-Hey, you.
-Hey.

1066
01:44:19,754 --> 01:44:23,506
Do you know that blue sport coat
that I never wear?

1067
01:44:23,591 --> 01:44:26,343
Hang on a sec, hon. I'm boiling over.

1068
01:44:32,350 --> 01:44:34,017
Now, what about the blue sport coat
you never wear?

1069
01:44:34,101 --> 01:44:36,353
Would you mind bringing it
to Arlington tomorrow?

1070
01:44:36,437 --> 01:44:39,648
Arlington? I thought you were in...

1071
01:44:40,775 --> 01:44:43,526
You tell me right now, Jimmy Morris.

1072
01:44:45,237 --> 01:44:49,366
Apparently, there's a dress code
in the major leagues.

1073
01:44:53,329 --> 01:44:55,789
-Lorri?
-Yeah, I'm here.

1074
01:44:58,501 --> 01:45:02,504
They're flying me out in an hour.
Can you tell everybody?

1075
01:45:02,588 --> 01:45:06,132
Sure. You just have to tell
one person first.

1076
01:45:06,217 --> 01:45:08,176
Hunter?

1077
01:45:08,260 --> 01:45:10,970
Your daddy's got something
he wants to tell you.

1078
01:45:12,640 --> 01:45:14,057
Hey, Dad.

1079
01:45:14,141 --> 01:45:18,436
-Hey, buddy. Hey, guess what?
-What?

1080
01:45:19,772 --> 01:45:22,190
Your daddy is gonna
be a major league pitcher.

1081
01:45:22,274 --> 01:45:23,358
Really?

1082
01:45:23,442 --> 01:45:26,528
Yeah, really.
What do you think about that, huh?

1083
01:45:26,654 --> 01:45:29,864
-Cool. I can't believe it.
-Well.

1084
01:45:29,949 --> 01:45:32,075
Who are you gonna play for?

1085
01:45:32,159 --> 01:45:35,370
-They're called the Devil Rays.
-What's a Devil Ray?

1086
01:45:35,663 --> 01:45:38,832
-lt's a fish.
-What colour is it?

1087
01:45:38,916 --> 01:45:41,042
-Black.
-Can you eat it?

1088
01:45:41,127 --> 01:45:43,294
l don't know, bud. lt's got a stinger.

1089
01:45:43,379 --> 01:45:47,841
That's cool.
My daddy's a big league pitcher.

1090
01:48:11,610 --> 01:48:13,653
Team won't be here
for a couple of hours.

1091
01:48:13,737 --> 01:48:15,947
Feel free to look around.

1092
01:48:20,953 --> 01:48:23,788
We ain't in Kansas any more,
are we, Riv?

1093
01:49:08,834 --> 01:49:10,335
Good evening, ladies and gentlemen,

1094
01:49:10,419 --> 01:49:13,421
and welcome
to the Ballpark in Arlington.

1095
01:49:13,505 --> 01:49:15,965
lt's baseball time in Texas.

1096
01:49:16,050 --> 01:49:18,343
Tonight's game,
the Tampa Bay Devil Rays

1097
01:49:18,427 --> 01:49:21,846
against your Texas Rangers.

1098
01:50:00,469 --> 01:50:03,638
Jimmy! Jimmy Morris!

1099
01:50:03,722 --> 01:50:04,806
Dad!

1100
01:50:10,479 --> 01:50:12,397
-Hi, Dad.
-Hi.

1101
01:50:15,609 --> 01:50:16,734
You look great.

1102
01:50:17,778 --> 01:50:19,696
Kids, doesn't your daddy look great?

1103
01:50:19,780 --> 01:50:21,155
-Yup.
-Yeah.

1104
01:50:24,284 --> 01:50:25,827
-Hey.
-Hey.

1105
01:50:26,286 --> 01:50:28,037
I can't believe it.

1106
01:50:30,165 --> 01:50:32,208
-Hey, sweetheart.
-Hi, Daddy.

1107
01:50:32,501 --> 01:50:34,127
-You're so big.
-Yeah.

1108
01:50:34,795 --> 01:50:36,254
Hey, partner.

1109
01:50:36,964 --> 01:50:38,881
-I missed you.
-Me, too.

1110
01:50:39,925 --> 01:50:43,594
Here are the starting lineups
for tonight's game.

1111
01:50:45,597 --> 01:50:48,307
Well, kids, we'd better let your daddy
get to work.

1112
01:50:49,643 --> 01:50:51,436
We'll see you after.

1113
01:50:59,236 --> 01:51:00,236
Okay.

1114
01:51:08,287 --> 01:51:11,080
Here at the Ballpark, the Devil Rays
went down one-two-three

1115
01:51:11,165 --> 01:51:13,166
in the top of the first,
and now the Rangers

1116
01:51:13,250 --> 01:51:16,419
try to get their high scoring offence
on track in the bottom of the first.

1117
01:51:16,503 --> 01:51:22,717
Leading off for the Rangers, centre
fielder, number 24, Tom Goodwin.

1118
01:51:41,695 --> 01:51:44,489
Three balls and a strike. One out.
The pitch on the way.

1119
01:51:44,573 --> 01:51:47,617
lt is low. Ball four.
Runners at first and second.

1120
01:51:47,701 --> 01:51:49,952
A scoring chance for Texas.

1121
01:51:50,037 --> 01:51:52,830
...page 24 in your
souvenir programme...

1122
01:51:52,915 --> 01:51:55,458
Here's the pitch.
lt is swung on and lined to centre field.

1123
01:51:55,542 --> 01:52:00,379
A base hit. A run will score.
Runners at first and second.

1124
01:52:00,464 --> 01:52:03,800
lt's one-nothing Texas, and the
Rangers have a one-to-nothing lead.

1125
01:52:03,884 --> 01:52:05,510
There's a swing
and a base hit to right field.

1126
01:52:05,594 --> 01:52:08,554
And this game is now a one-one tie.

1127
01:52:11,725 --> 01:52:13,267
A reminder, the Devil Rays and
Rangers play again.

1128
01:52:13,352 --> 01:52:15,603
Last half of the fourth inning about to
get underway.

1129
01:52:15,687 --> 01:52:18,564
lt's the Rangers one
and the Devil Rays one.

1130
01:52:21,944 --> 01:52:23,861
The pitch in the dirt again.

1131
01:52:23,946 --> 01:52:27,115
The third one he has thrown.
And we're about to get some action,

1132
01:52:27,199 --> 01:52:28,658
it appears, in the Tampa Bay bullpen.

1133
01:52:28,742 --> 01:52:30,993
Blanton, Miller.

1134
01:52:33,122 --> 01:52:34,455
No, you ain't.

1135
01:52:36,917 --> 01:52:39,335
The pitch is on the way.
Swung on, and a shot to the gap

1136
01:52:39,419 --> 01:52:42,004
in right centre field.
lt looks like extra bases.

1137
01:52:42,089 --> 01:52:44,340
One run is home. Two runs are home.

1138
01:52:44,424 --> 01:52:47,969
The throw cut off. The play at third.
Out at third base!

1139
01:52:48,262 --> 01:52:51,764
Rangers three, Devil Rays one,
here in the bottom of the sixth.

1140
01:52:51,849 --> 01:52:55,852
On this day in baseball history.

1141
01:52:55,936 --> 01:52:59,188
Runners at first and second.
A golden chance for the Rangers.

1142
01:52:59,273 --> 01:53:03,401
Here's the set. The pitch on the way.
A swing and a smash

1143
01:53:03,485 --> 01:53:07,155
up the alley in left centre field.
That will score two.

1144
01:53:07,239 --> 01:53:09,991
lt's the Rangers five,
the Devil Rays one.

1145
01:53:10,075 --> 01:53:11,909
Texas has broken it open.

1146
01:53:18,625 --> 01:53:21,294
The Devil Rays and Rangers also play
on Sunday night.

1147
01:53:21,378 --> 01:53:24,213
Game time is 7:05, and it's Cap Night.

1148
01:53:24,298 --> 01:53:26,549
All fans thirteen and under
receive a Rangers cap.

1149
01:53:26,633 --> 01:53:29,677
Game time Sunday is 7:05.

1150
01:53:31,513 --> 01:53:32,680
Morris!

1151
01:53:33,348 --> 01:53:35,266
Start warming up.

1152
01:53:40,939 --> 01:53:42,190
Mommy.

1153
01:53:42,274 --> 01:53:44,442
He's down in the bullpen.

1154
01:53:44,943 --> 01:53:46,944
Oh, my Lord.

1155
01:53:50,782 --> 01:53:54,243
Hey. Hey guys. There he is.
There he is. There he is.

1156
01:53:54,328 --> 01:53:56,996
Look, he's in the bullpen.
There he is. Yes.

1157
01:53:57,080 --> 01:53:58,623
He looks good.

1158
01:54:02,377 --> 01:54:04,545
Come here.
Here, here. Have a looksee.

1159
01:54:05,756 --> 01:54:08,007
Blanton trying to get out of
a bases loaded jam.

1160
01:54:08,091 --> 01:54:10,051
There's a swing
and a smash right back at him.

1161
01:54:10,135 --> 01:54:12,720
He retrieves the ball in front of the...

1162
01:54:12,804 --> 01:54:17,225
And he throws it away.
Another run will come home.

1163
01:54:17,309 --> 01:54:19,644
Two runs for Texas on the play,

1164
01:54:19,728 --> 01:54:22,063
and the Rangers
have busted this game wide open.

1165
01:54:22,648 --> 01:54:27,026
And Blanton may be hurt
on that rocket right back to the mound.

1166
01:54:27,110 --> 01:54:29,528
Jim Morris continues
to throw in the bullpen.

1167
01:54:29,613 --> 01:54:30,988
The left-hander from Big Lake, Texas.

1168
01:54:31,073 --> 01:54:32,823
Lookit. It's Coach. It's Coach.

1169
01:54:36,912 --> 01:54:41,123
-Let's go! Come on!
-Let's go, Coach!

1170
01:54:41,208 --> 01:54:44,210
The Tampa Bay trainer is on his way
out to the mound to check.

1171
01:54:44,294 --> 01:54:47,421
lt appears the Devil Rays
might have to make a pitching change.

1172
01:54:47,506 --> 01:54:50,591
Morris! You're in.

1173
01:55:22,416 --> 01:55:24,375
Let's go, Coach!

1174
01:55:49,401 --> 01:55:53,362
Just three months ago,
he was grading chemistry tests,

1175
01:55:53,447 --> 01:55:58,451
and now Jim Morris finds himself on
the mound in a major league ballpark.

1176
01:55:59,369 --> 01:56:04,540
Little louder than back home?
Well, then let's not make this too hard.

1177
01:56:05,125 --> 01:56:08,377
You remember that fast ball you were
showing me before the game?

1178
01:56:08,462 --> 01:56:12,214
-I think so.
-Well, I need three of them.

1179
01:56:12,299 --> 01:56:14,550
Coming in to pitch for Tampa Bay,

1180
01:56:14,634 --> 01:56:19,722
a fellow Texan making
his major league debut, Jim Morris.

1181
01:56:21,975 --> 01:56:24,685
Morris will be facing Ranger shortstop,
Royce Clayton.

1182
01:56:24,770 --> 01:56:27,104
Royce has had
a very strong second half,

1183
01:56:27,189 --> 01:56:31,025
and has not cooled off any
here in the month of September.

1184
01:56:31,485 --> 01:56:32,985
Come on, Dad!

1185
01:57:02,516 --> 01:57:04,350
-Yeah!
-Yes!

1186
01:57:08,355 --> 01:57:10,481
One more time!

1187
01:57:22,744 --> 01:57:24,036
Strike two!

1188
01:58:39,988 --> 01:58:43,324
Jimmy, what pitches did you throw
to get that strikeout?

1189
01:58:48,288 --> 01:58:53,751
Fast ball, fast ball and fast ball.

1190
01:58:54,127 --> 01:58:56,295
How'd it feel?
Pitching in the major leagues?

1191
01:58:56,463 --> 01:58:59,215
-Just like I hoped it would.
-How does the arm feel?

1192
01:59:06,765 --> 01:59:11,310
Could you excuse me for... Just for
one second? Could you excuse me?

1193
01:59:19,319 --> 01:59:22,780
Sir, I didn't know you were here.

1194
01:59:25,575 --> 01:59:27,535
Wasn't missing this one.

1195
01:59:34,251 --> 01:59:36,252
Watching you tonight.

1196
01:59:42,676 --> 01:59:45,052
Not many fathers
get a chance to do that.

1197
01:59:50,100 --> 01:59:53,143
I guess I let too many of those things
get away.

1198
01:59:59,359 --> 02:00:00,651
So did I.

1199
02:00:06,908 --> 02:00:08,701
Well, then.

1200
02:00:09,286 --> 02:00:11,620
-Hey, Dad.
-Yeah?

1201
02:00:32,350 --> 02:00:34,935
Thanks for coming.

1202
02:01:13,558 --> 02:01:16,185
Does this mean I don't get a baseball?

1203
02:01:25,987 --> 02:01:29,615
-That's all I got left.
-Well, a girl could do worse.

1204
02:01:35,288 --> 02:01:38,666
So how does it feel being
the oldest rookie in the last 30 years?

1205
02:01:39,584 --> 02:01:43,128
Well, I don't know. I'm tired.

1206
02:01:44,964 --> 02:01:46,507
So where are the kids?

1207
02:01:46,591 --> 02:01:49,802
Hunter is with Henry,
and Jessie's with Cal.

1208
02:01:49,886 --> 02:01:54,556
-Don't tell me you left Jessie with Cal.
-Cal had a little help.


